-- modules (content = lib/types/curriculum.ts Module as JSON)
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('markets', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 1, '{"id":"markets","sequence":1,"title":"Reading a market snapshot","summary":"Price, 24-hour change, volume, market capitalisation, liquidity and volatility: what each number measures, what it does not, and how to say so in plain words.","learningObjectives":["Read a price and a 24-hour percentage change and describe them without turning them into a forecast.","Explain why volume measures activity and cannot show whether buyers or sellers dominated.","Compute market capitalisation from price and circulating supply and explain why it is not money invested.","Separate liquidity (the cost of trading now) from volume (what traded in the past) and from volatility (how much price moves).","Describe how crypto prices arise across many venues and why two quotes for the same asset can differ.","Tell a 24/7 crypto quote from a market-hours quote, and say when a displayed price is stale."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":[],"skillIds":["markets.price","markets.volume","markets.market-cap","markets.liquidity","markets.volatility","markets.structure","markets.trading-hours"],"concepts":[{"heading":"A price is a record, not a verdict","body":"A quoted price is the price of the most recent trade, or the midpoint between the best bid and the best ask. Either way, it records one agreement between one buyer and one seller at one moment. It is not a measurement of what the asset is worth. It is a fact about a trade.\n\nThe **24-hour change** compares the current price with the price 24 hours earlier. It is two points joined by a line. It does not show the path between them, how many trades happened, or what will happen next. A +3% day and a day that rose 8% and then fell 5% both print as +3%.\n\nWhen you read a snapshot, write the observation first and keep it plain: \"BTC is quoted at X, Y% above its price 24 hours ago.\" Then, if you want to interpret, do that in a separate sentence and say it is your reading.\n\n- Price: what the last trade cost.\n- 24h change: current price against one earlier point.\n- Neither one is a forecast, and neither one is a value.","example":"Suppose BTC is quoted at 60,000 with a 24-hour change of +2.5%. The observation is: price is 60,000, about 1,460 above where it was yesterday. It does not follow that BTC is ''worth'' 60,000, that it is ''going up'', or that buyers were ''in control''. Those are interpretations you would need other evidence for."},{"heading":"Volume measures activity, not direction","body":"**Volume** is the total amount traded over a window, usually 24 hours, measured either in units of the asset or in the quote currency (dollars). Data providers often call the dollar figure **turnover**.\n\nEvery trade has exactly one buyer and one seller. If 1,000 BTC changed hands, then 1,000 BTC were bought and 1,000 BTC were sold. So volume cannot tell you whether \"buyers\" or \"sellers\" dominated. It tells you how much changed hands.\n\nWhat volume can do is show how unusual today''s activity is. The useful comparison is with the asset''s own recent history: is today''s volume near its 30-day average, or two or three times higher? High volume on a large price move suggests a lot of people repriced the asset at once. Low volume on a large move suggests the move happened with few participants, which can mean a thin market.\n\n- Always compare volume with its own recent average, not with another asset.\n- Say \"volume was high\" and stop. Do not say \"buying volume was high\".\n- Check whether the figure is in units or in dollars, and which venues it covers.","example":"Suppose ETH shows 24-hour volume of 12 billion dollars against a 30-day average of 8 billion, with price up 4%. A sound observation is: ''volume was about 50% above its recent average on a 4% rise''. An unsound one is: ''strong buying volume pushed ETH up''. The data shows activity. It does not show which side wanted it more."},{"heading":"Market capitalisation is a label for size","body":"**Market capitalisation** (market cap) is the current price multiplied by the circulating supply. If a coin trades at 2 and there are 500 million in circulation, its market cap is 1 billion.\n\nBecause it is a multiplication, market cap moves whenever price moves, even if no new money arrives. One small trade at a 1% higher price lifts the whole figure by 1%. That is why \"X billion was wiped out\" headlines are misleading: nobody lost that cash, because that cash never existed as a pool. Market cap is a way of ranking and comparing size, not a count of money invested.\n\nRelated fields you will see in a snapshot:\n\n- **Circulating supply**: coins available to trade now.\n- **Total supply** and **max supply**: coins that exist, and coins that could ever exist. Max supply can be unlimited.\n- **Fully diluted valuation (FDV)**: price multiplied by max (or total) supply. A large gap between FDV and market cap means a lot of supply is still to come.\n- **Market cap rank**: position among all assets by market cap.","example":"Suppose a token has price 4, circulating supply 250 million and max supply 1 billion. Market cap is 1 billion; FDV is 4 billion. If price rises to 4.40, market cap becomes 1.1 billion. No 100 million of new money arrived; the price of the last trade changed and the multiplication followed it."},{"heading":"Liquidity and volatility are different things","body":"**Liquidity** is how cheaply you can trade a given size right now. It shows up as a narrow gap between the best bid and best ask, and as plenty of resting orders close to the current price. It is a property of the market at this moment.\n\n**Volume** is a property of the past: what traded over a window. A market can have high volume over the last 24 hours and a thin order book right now. The two often go together, but they are not the same measurement, and you should not use one as a stand-in for the other.\n\n**Volatility** is how much price moves over time. One simple proxy is the 24-hour high minus the 24-hour low as a percentage of price. Volatility has no sign: a market that rose 6% and one that fell 6% were equally volatile. It is not good or bad on its own. What matters is whether today''s range is normal for this asset. BTC moving 2% in a day is ordinary; a stablecoin moving 2% is extraordinary.\n\n- Liquidity: cost to trade now (spread and depth).\n- Volume: amount traded in the past window.\n- Volatility: size of price moves over time, compared with the asset''s own normal range.","example":"Suppose a snapshot shows a 24-hour high of 62,000 and low of 58,000 with price at 60,000. The day''s range is about 6.7% of price. Before calling that ''wild'', compare it with the asset''s usual daily range. If recent days ranged 2 to 3%, today is unusual. If they ranged 5 to 8%, today is ordinary."},{"heading":"Where a crypto price comes from","body":"There is no single place where \"the\" bitcoin price is set. BTC trades on many venues at once: centralised exchanges, on-chain order books and automated market makers. Each venue has its own order book and its own last trade. A data provider''s price is usually an average or a volume-weighted blend across venues.\n\nAssets are quoted against a **quote currency**: a stablecoin such as USDC or USDT, or a fiat currency. \"BTC/USDC\" means the price of one BTC in USDC. Two quotes for the same asset can differ by a little because they come from different venues at slightly different times, or because the quote currencies themselves differ slightly from one dollar.\n\nThere are also **spot** markets, where the asset itself changes hands, and **derivative** markets, where contracts referencing the asset trade. Derivative volume is often many times larger than spot volume. A snapshot usually shows spot figures, so it describes only part of the activity.\n\n- Ask which venues and which quote currency a price covers.\n- Expect small differences between sources; large differences deserve a check of timestamps.\n- Remember that spot data leaves out derivatives.","example":"Suppose one provider quotes BTC at 60,010 and an on-chain order book shows a last trade at 59,985. Both can be correct: the first is a blended figure across venues as of one timestamp; the second is a single venue''s last trade a few seconds earlier. The right description is ''quotes differ by about 0.04% across sources'', not ''one of them is wrong''."},{"heading":"Trading hours: 24/7 quotes and market-hours quotes","body":"Crypto spot markets trade all day, every day. There is no closing bell, so a BTC quote at 03:00 on a Sunday is a real price that someone could trade at.\n\nA **tokenised ETF** is different. Its share tracks a fund listed on a stock exchange, and the fund''s own price only moves while that exchange is open. Outside those hours the board may show one of several things:\n\n- **Pre-market** or **after-hours**: thin early or late trading, often with wide gaps between prints.\n- **Closed**: the last official close, which can be many hours old.\n- A provider estimate, labelled with its source.\n\nSo a price on a screen comes with two questions: **when was it set**, and **is the market it follows open right now**? A row that says \"pre-market\" next to an as-of time from last night is not the price you would get at the next open. Some providers also flag rows as stale or unavailable; read that flag before the number.\n\nWhen markets reopen, the price can jump to reflect everything that happened while it was shut. That gap is not a crash or a rally that \"happened at the open\"; it is the catch-up of a price that could not move.\n\nHabit: always write \"last price X as of <time>, market <status>\" for anything that is not 24/7.","example":"Suppose a board shows a tokenised ETF at 100.00 with status ''closed'' and an as-of time of 21:00 yesterday, next to BTC at 60,000 as of 09:30 today. The BTC figure is a live 24/7 quote; the ETF figure is a twelve-hour-old close. If the fund''s home market opens at 101.50, that 1.5% jump is the catch-up of a price that was frozen overnight."}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.markets.price.1","skillId":"markets.price","prompt":"A snapshot shows BTC at 60,000 with a 24-hour change of +2.5%. Which statement is an observation rather than an interpretation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"BTC is now worth 60,000.","correct":false,"explanation":"This treats the last traded price as a value judgement. Price records what a trade cost, not what the asset is worth.","misconceptionId":"price-equals-value"},{"id":"b","text":"BTC is quoted at 60,000, about 2.5% above its price 24 hours ago.","correct":true,"explanation":"This restates the two data points without adding a claim about value, direction or cause. That is what an observation looks like."},{"id":"c","text":"BTC is trending up and should keep rising.","correct":false,"explanation":"A single 24-hour change is two points in time. It says nothing about the path or what comes next.","misconceptionId":"percent-change-is-trend"},{"id":"d","text":"Buyers were in control over the last 24 hours.","correct":false,"explanation":"Every trade has one buyer and one seller. A price rise shows trades happened at higher prices, not that one side was ''in control''.","misconceptionId":"volume-implies-direction"}]},{"id":"kc.markets.volume.1","skillId":"markets.volume","prompt":"ETH''s 24-hour volume is 12 billion dollars against a 30-day average of 8 billion, and price rose 4%. What can you conclude from the volume figure alone?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Buyers outnumbered sellers by a wide margin.","correct":false,"explanation":"Volume is always balanced: each unit bought is a unit sold. It cannot show which side dominated.","misconceptionId":"volume-implies-direction"},{"id":"b","text":"Activity was about 50% above its recent average.","correct":true,"explanation":"Comparing today''s volume with the asset''s own recent average is the one sound inference. It tells you more changed hands than usual, nothing more."},{"id":"c","text":"ETH is more liquid than usual right now.","correct":false,"explanation":"Volume describes the past window. Liquidity is the cost of trading now, which needs spread and depth, not volume.","misconceptionId":"liquidity-equals-volume"},{"id":"d","text":"The rise will continue because volume confirms it.","correct":false,"explanation":"''Volume confirms the move'' is a slogan, not a measurement. Volume shows how much traded, and no amount of it forecasts the next move.","misconceptionId":"volume-implies-direction"}]},{"id":"kc.markets.market-cap.1","skillId":"markets.market-cap","prompt":"A token has price 4, circulating supply 250 million and max supply 1 billion. Price rises to 4.40. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"100 million dollars of new money flowed into the token.","correct":false,"explanation":"Market cap rose by 100 million because price times supply rose. No cash pool grew; a trade at a higher price changed the multiplication.","misconceptionId":"market-cap-is-money-invested"},{"id":"b","text":"Market cap is now 1.1 billion and FDV is 4.4 billion.","correct":true,"explanation":"Market cap = 4.40 × 250 million = 1.1 billion. FDV = 4.40 × 1 billion = 4.4 billion. Both followed the price mechanically."},{"id":"c","text":"FDV is now 1.1 billion.","correct":false,"explanation":"FDV uses max (or total) supply, not circulating supply. 4.40 × 1 billion is 4.4 billion."},{"id":"d","text":"Market cap cannot change unless supply changes.","correct":false,"explanation":"Market cap is price multiplied by supply, so it moves whenever price moves, even with supply fixed.","misconceptionId":"market-cap-is-money-invested"}]},{"id":"kc.markets.volatility.1","skillId":"markets.volatility","prompt":"BTC''s 24-hour high is 62,000 and low is 58,000, with price at 60,000. Recent daily ranges were 2 to 3%. What is the best description?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Volatility is dangerous and the market is unstable.","correct":false,"explanation":"Volatility is a measurement with no sign. ''Dangerous'' and ''unstable'' are judgements that the range figure alone does not support.","misconceptionId":"volatility-is-bad"},{"id":"b","text":"Today''s range is about 6.7% of price, roughly two to three times the recent norm.","correct":true,"explanation":"(62,000 − 58,000) / 60,000 ≈ 6.7%. Comparing with the asset''s own recent range is what makes ''unusual'' a defensible word."},{"id":"c","text":"The market is highly liquid because it moved a lot.","correct":false,"explanation":"A wide range says nothing about the cost of trading now. Liquidity needs spread and depth, not the day''s high and low.","misconceptionId":"liquidity-equals-volume"},{"id":"d","text":"BTC is now worth less because it was volatile.","correct":false,"explanation":"Volatility describes movement. It does not tell you anything about value, which is a separate concept from price in any case.","misconceptionId":"price-equals-value"}]},{"id":"kc.markets.trading-hours.1","skillId":"markets.trading-hours","prompt":"At 08:00 on a Saturday, a board shows BTC at 60,000 (as of 08:00) and a tokenised ETF at 250.00 with status ''closed'' (as of 21:00 Friday). Which statement is most accurate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Both are current prices you could trade at right now.","correct":false,"explanation":"BTC trades 24/7, so its 08:00 quote is current. The ETF''s home market is shut; 250.00 is Friday''s close, eleven hours old.","misconceptionId":"stale-price-is-current"},{"id":"b","text":"BTC''s quote is current; the ETF figure is Friday''s last price and may jump when its market reopens.","correct":true,"explanation":"Reading the status and as-of time first tells you which number is live and which is frozen until the next session."},{"id":"c","text":"The ETF is safer because its price is not moving this weekend.","correct":false,"explanation":"The price is not moving because the market is shut, not because risk has gone. News over the weekend shows up as a gap at the open."},{"id":"d","text":"The ETF price is wrong because it is older than the BTC price.","correct":false,"explanation":"It is not wrong; it is the correct last price for a market that is closed. It just must be labelled with its time and status."}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.markets.describe-snapshot","skillIds":["markets.price","markets.volume","markets.market-cap"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Describe a snapshot without forecasting","difficulty":1,"learningObjective":"Restate price, 24-hour change, volume and market cap as plain observations and keep interpretation separate.","evidenceRequirements":["snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH"],"questions":["For BTC and ETH, state the price, the 24-hour change and the 24-hour volume as plain observations. What exactly does each number measure?","Which of the two assets changed more over 24 hours, and what can you not conclude from that comparison?","What would you need in addition to these snapshots to say whether today''s volume is unusual?"],"expectedReasoning":["Separates observation from interpretation in distinct sentences","Explains volume as activity with one buyer and one seller per trade","Explains market cap as price multiplied by circulating supply","Names a comparison baseline (recent average) as missing"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["States each asset''s price and 24-hour change correctly from the snapshot","Describes the 24-hour change as a comparison of two points in time","Describes volume as total traded, not as buying or selling","Describes market cap as price multiplied by circulating supply","Does not present any figure as a value judgement or forecast"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the actual figures from both snapshots","Notes the timestamp and provenance of the data","Uses the figures to compare the two assets rather than describing them in isolation","Does not invent figures that are not in the pack"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why a larger 24-hour change does not mean a stronger trend","Explains why volume cannot show which side dominated","Keeps interpretation, if any, clearly labelled as the learner''s reading","Draws only conclusions the two snapshots can support"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Names the missing baseline (recent average volume or range) needed to judge ''unusual''","Acknowledges that the snapshot covers spot data from the provider''s venues only","Avoids words such as ''clearly'' or ''definitely'' where the data does not support them","Says what cannot be concluded from a 24-hour comparison"]}],"keyPoints":["price is the last traded or blended quote at one timestamp","24-hour change compares two points in time and does not show the path","volume counts units or dollars traded and every trade has one buyer and one seller","market cap equals price multiplied by circulating supply","a larger percentage change is not evidence of a stronger trend","judging volume as unusual needs the asset''s own recent average","the snapshot covers spot markets only"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Comparing volume with the other asset''s volume as a size comparison, as long as it is not used to judge ''unusual''","Describing the change in dollars rather than percent","Noting that the two snapshots may have slightly different timestamps"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["price-equals-value","volume-implies-direction","percent-change-is-trend","market-cap-is-money-invested"]},"hints":["Start each sentence with the number and what it measures. Leave out any word that describes a cause or a future.","Remember that every trade has one buyer and one seller. What does that mean for any sentence that says ''buyers'' or ''sellers''?","A full answer reads like: ''BTC is quoted at X at time T, Y% above 24 hours earlier; 24h volume was Z. I cannot say whether this is unusual without the recent average.''"]},{"id":"ex.markets.liquidity-vs-volume","skillIds":["markets.liquidity","markets.volume","markets.volatility"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Liquidity, volume and volatility: which is which?","difficulty":1,"learningObjective":"Use a snapshot and an order book to show that volume, liquidity and volatility are three separate measurements.","evidenceRequirements":["snapshot:BTC","orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC"],"questions":["From the snapshot, what is BTC''s 24-hour volume? From the order book, what is the spread as a percentage of the mid price? Which of these describes liquidity, and why?","If the 24-hour volume were twice as high but the order book looked the same, would BTC be more liquid right now? Explain.","What one extra piece of data would let you say whether today''s price range was volatile for BTC?"],"expectedReasoning":["Defines liquidity as the cost of trading a given size now","Defines volume as past activity over a window","Uses spread from the order book as a liquidity measure","Treats volatility as a comparison with the asset''s own normal range"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Reads the 24-hour volume from the snapshot correctly","Computes or reads the spread and expresses it as a share of the mid price","Identifies spread and depth, not volume, as the liquidity measure","Describes volatility as the size of price moves, with no sign","States that doubling past volume does not change the current book"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites specific figures from the snapshot and the order book","Notes that the order book and the snapshot come from different providers and times","Uses the book''s best bid and best ask rather than a vague ''tight'' or ''wide''","Does not use volume as a stand-in for either liquidity or volatility"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains the difference between a past window and a present snapshot","Explains why volume and liquidity often move together but are not the same","Reasons about what a doubled volume with an unchanged book would and would not mean","Identifies the recent range as the right baseline for volatility"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Notes that the order book is a snapshot that may change before any trade","Notes that the order book is one venue while the snapshot blends several","Says that without a recent range the volatility question cannot be answered","Avoids overstating what a single spread reading shows"]}],"keyPoints":["liquidity is the cost of trading a given size now","spread as a share of mid price measures the cost of immediacy","volume is what traded over the past 24 hours","doubling past volume does not change the current order book","volatility is the size of price moves compared with the asset''s normal range","the order book is one venue and the snapshot is a blend","a snapshot of the book may change before a trade arrives"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using depth near the mid instead of spread as the primary liquidity measure","Expressing spread in ticks rather than percent, if the percentage is also implied","Using the 24-hour high and low as a rough volatility proxy, with the caveat that a baseline is still needed"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["liquidity-equals-volume","volatility-is-bad","depth-is-static"]},"hints":["One of these numbers is about the past, one is about right now, and one is about movement. Sort them first.","The spread is (best ask − best bid). Divide it by the mid price to get a percentage. Which concept does that percentage belong to?","A full answer says: volume is past activity; spread/depth is current liquidity; volatility needs today''s range compared with recent ranges, which the pack does not include."]},{"id":"ex.markets.two-quotes","skillIds":["markets.structure","markets.price"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Why do two quotes for BTC differ?","difficulty":2,"learningObjective":"Explain small differences between a blended price and a single-venue order book using market structure, not error.","evidenceRequirements":["snapshot:BTC","orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC"],"questions":["Compare the snapshot price for BTC with the mid price of the order book. How far apart are they, in percent?","Give at least two structural reasons the two figures can differ without either being wrong.","What would make you suspect that one of the figures is stale or faulty rather than just different?"],"expectedReasoning":["Computes the difference as a percentage","Names venue, quote currency and timestamp as sources of difference","Distinguishes a normal spread between sources from a stale or faulty feed","Uses provenance timestamps as evidence"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Computes the gap between snapshot price and order book mid correctly","States that the snapshot is a blended or provider price and the book is one venue","States that the book is quoted in a stablecoin that may not be exactly one dollar","Recognises that timestamps differ between the two sources","Does not claim one source is the ''real'' price"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites both figures and both timestamps from the pack","Uses the provenance tags (live, cached, demo) in the reasoning","Notes the venue''s displayed market name and quote asset","Uses the spread of the book to put the difference in context"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why many venues produce many prices at once","Explains how a blended price is built from several venues","Sets a rough threshold for when a gap deserves investigation","Links a stale timestamp or a halted market to the possibility of a faulty figure"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Acknowledges not knowing exactly how the provider blends its price","Notes that the book snapshot could be seconds old","Says what extra data (more venues, exact timestamps) would settle the question","Avoids declaring an error without evidence"]}],"keyPoints":["there is no single bitcoin price because many venues trade it at once","a provider price is a blend across venues at one timestamp","the order book mid is one venue at its block time","the quote currency is a stablecoin that may not equal exactly one dollar","a small gap between sources is normal","a large gap or an old timestamp suggests a stale or faulty feed","provenance tags show whether data is live, cached or demo"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the order book''s best bid or best ask instead of the mid for the comparison, if stated","Treating a gap wider than the book''s own spread as the threshold for ''worth checking''","Mentioning derivative markets as another reason prices can lead or lag"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["price-equals-value","mid-price-is-tradeable"]},"hints":["Look at where each number comes from and when. The snapshot and the book are not from the same place.","Think about: different venues, different quote currencies, different timestamps, and whether a price is blended or single-trade.","A full answer computes the gap, lists venue, quote asset and timestamp as reasons, and says that a gap much wider than the book''s own spread or a timestamp hours old would make you suspect a stale feed."]},{"id":"ex.markets.trading-hours","skillIds":["markets.trading-hours","markets.structure"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Which prices are live?","difficulty":2,"learningObjective":"Sort the prices in a pack into live 24/7 quotes and market-hours quotes, read each one''s status and time, and say what a stale price can and cannot tell you.","evidenceRequirements":["wealth:board","snapshot:BTC"],"questions":["From the fund board, list three funds and give each one''s market status, as-of time and price source. Which of them follow a market that keeps exchange hours?","Compare the as-of time of the BTC snapshot with the as-of time of one tokenised ETF row. How old is each figure relative to the pack''s creation time?","If a market-hours fund is in pre-market or closed, what could happen to its price at the next open, and why is that not a move that ''happened'' at the open?","Write one sentence that reports a market-hours fund''s price honestly, with its status and time."],"expectedReasoning":["Reads market status and as-of time before the price","Separates 24/7 crypto quotes from exchange-hours fund quotes","Explains the opening gap as catch-up of a frozen price","Reports a stale price with its time and status"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Reads the market status of each chosen fund correctly from the board","Reads the as-of times correctly and computes their age against the pack time","States that crypto spot trades 24/7 while tokenised ETFs follow their home market''s hours","Explains that a closed or pre-market price may not be the next tradeable price","Does not describe an exchange-hours price as live when the status says otherwise"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites fund ids and their market status from the board","Cites the price source or price status field where present","Cites the BTC snapshot''s timestamp","Notes the provenance of each figure: live, cached or demo"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why the same screen can mix live and frozen prices","Explains the opening gap as information arriving while the market was shut","Separates what the row shows from what the next open might bring","Writes an honest one-line report that includes status and time"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says the next opening price cannot be known from the pack","Notes that pre-market prints can be thin and unrepresentative","Flags any row whose price status is not ready","Avoids forecasting the direction of any opening gap"]}],"keyPoints":["crypto spot markets trade around the clock","tokenised etfs follow the hours of their home market","read the market status and as-of time before the price","a closed market shows a last price that can be hours old","prices can gap at the open to catch up","report a stale price with its time and status"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Choosing escrow vault rows instead of tokenised ETFs, if their status is shown","Measuring age in minutes or hours, as long as it is consistent","Describing pre-market as ''thin early trading'' without naming the exchange"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["stale-price-is-current","price-equals-value","percent-change-is-trend"]},"hints":["Find the market status and the as-of time on each row before you look at the price.","Subtract each as-of time from the pack''s creation time. Which figures are minutes old and which are hours old, and why?","A full answer lists funds with status, time and source; contrasts them with the 24/7 BTC quote; explains the opening gap as catch-up; and writes ''last price X as of <time>, market <status>''."]}],"rubricNotes":"This module rewards plain description. Full marks on ''factual'' need the right numbers and the right definitions; full marks on ''reasoning'' need a visible gap between what was seen and what is inferred. Penalise any sentence that reads a price as a value, volume as a side, or a 24-hour change as a trend. Do not penalise a learner for declining to interpret.","recommendedNext":"Move to ''Reading the news'' to learn how to split headlines into facts, claims and opinions before you connect them to the numbers you can now read."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('news', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 2, '{"id":"news","sequence":2,"title":"Reading the news","summary":"How to take a news item apart: what is a fact, what is a claim, who said it, what event it points to, and why ''price moved on the news'' is almost never something you can prove.","learningObjectives":["Split a news item into verifiable facts, attributed claims and the writer''s opinion.","Identify a catalyst, state the expected outcome, and say what a surprise would look like.","Recognise a narrative as a story that organises facts and test whether the facts hold it up.","Rate a claim by its sourcing and keep single-source claims marked as unconfirmed.","Check timing, size and mechanism before linking a headline to a price move, and name at least one alternative explanation."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":["markets"],"skillIds":["news.facts-vs-opinions","news.catalysts","news.narratives","news.uncertainty","news.causation"],"concepts":[{"heading":"Facts, claims and opinions","body":"A news item is a mix of three kinds of sentence. A **fact** is something you could check against a record: a filing was made, a block was mined, a price printed. A **claim** is something a person or organisation says is true, which may or may not be checkable: \"the firm expects approval next quarter.\" An **opinion** is the writer''s or a quoted person''s judgement: \"this is the most important development of the year.\"\n\nHeadlines compress all three into a few words and usually drop the attribution. \"Regulator to approve ETF\" might rest on a filing (fact), an unnamed source (claim) or a columnist''s guess (opinion). You cannot tell from the headline. You have to open the item.\n\nA simple method: copy the key sentence, then ask three questions.\n\n- Could I verify this against a document or a dataset? If yes, it is a fact.\n- Is someone named as saying it? If yes, it is a claim by that person.\n- Is it a judgement word (important, bullish, inevitable)? If yes, it is an opinion.\n\nWrite your summary with the attribution kept in: \"The exchange said it will list X\" rather than \"X will be listed.\"","example":"Suppose a headline reads ''Major fund set to buy 1 billion of BTC''. The body says ''a person familiar with the matter said the fund is considering'' it. The fact is that a news outlet published this. The claim is from an unnamed source and the verb is ''considering'', not ''set to''. There is no filing. Your note should say: ''reported, single anonymous source, intention not action, unconfirmed''."},{"heading":"Catalysts: events that could change expectations","body":"A **catalyst** is an event that could change what market participants expect. Some are scheduled: a central bank rate decision, an inflation print, a court ruling date, a token unlock, an ETF decision deadline. Some are unscheduled: an exchange outage, a hack, a surprise announcement.\n\nA catalyst does not guarantee a price move. Prices already reflect what people expect. If an event lands exactly as expected, there may be no move at all. What moves price is the gap between the outcome and the expectation. So, for each catalyst, write three things: when it happens, what outcome is expected, and what a surprise in either direction would look like.\n\nThe macro calendar in your evidence pack lists scheduled events by date. Use it to answer \"is anything scheduled in the next few days that could matter?\" rather than \"what will happen?\".\n\n- Scheduled catalysts: date known, outcome unknown.\n- Unscheduled catalysts: neither known in advance.\n- The important quantity is outcome minus expectation, not the event itself.","example":"Suppose the macro calendar shows an inflation release tomorrow, with a consensus forecast of 3.1% and the previous reading at 3.4%. The catalyst is the release. The expectation is 3.1%. A surprise would be a print well above or below that, say 3.5% or 2.7%. A print of 3.1% would be a catalyst that changed nothing."},{"heading":"Narratives organise facts; they are not facts","body":"A **narrative** is a story people use to make sense of many facts at once: \"institutions are coming\", \"regulation is tightening\", \"this is altcoin season\". Narratives are useful because they compress. They are dangerous because they feel like evidence while being a frame.\n\nThe test for a narrative is to list the specific observations it rests on and check each one. \"Institutions are coming\" might rest on: ETF net inflows have been positive for most of the last month; a bank announced a custody product; a survey said allocators plan to add. Each of those is checkable on its own. Some may hold, some may not, and some may be weaker than the story implies (a survey of intentions is not a flow).\n\nAlso notice that the same facts can support more than one narrative. Positive ETF inflows fit \"institutions are coming\" and also fit \"retail buying through brokerage accounts\". The facts do not pick the story for you.\n\n- Name the narrative.\n- List the two or three facts it leans on.\n- Check each fact and note which ones are strong.\n- Ask which other story the same facts could fit.","example":"Suppose three articles in your pack all mention ''growing institutional demand''. The observations behind them are: one week of positive ETF net inflows and one bank press release. That is thin. Your note might say: ''narrative: institutional demand. Support: positive flows over five sessions, one custody announcement. Not supported: any data on who is buying. Also fits: ordinary brokerage demand.''"},{"heading":"Sourcing: how sure can you be?","body":"Not all claims deserve the same confidence. Rank by source:\n\n- **Primary document**: a regulatory filing, a court order, an on-chain transaction, a company''s own announcement on its own channel. Highest confidence, though still check the date and the exact wording.\n- **Named official or executive on the record**: strong, but it is still that person''s claim.\n- **Reputable outlet citing unnamed sources**: a lead. It may be right. Keep it marked \"reported, unconfirmed\" until a document or a second independent outlet appears.\n- **Social media post, repost, screenshot**: lowest confidence until traced to its origin.\n\nTwo outlets repeating the same wire story is one source, not two. Independence means the outlets got the information separately.\n\nA news feed''s metadata can help: the publisher, the publish time, the link to the original, and which assets the item mentions. A missing original link, or a title that is null, is itself a signal to go carefully.","example":"Suppose your pack has two items saying a large exchange is under investigation. Item one cites ''people familiar with the matter''. Item two links to item one. That is one anonymous source. Your confidence note: ''single anonymous source, no filing, two outlets but not independent; treat as unconfirmed''. If item two had instead linked to a regulator''s press release, your confidence would rise sharply."},{"heading":"The headline did not necessarily cause the move","body":"When a headline and a price move land close together, the natural sentence is \"price fell on the news\". Resist it. Three things must fit before you can even suggest a cause.\n\n**Timing.** Which came first, to the minute? If price moved an hour before the item was published, the item did not cause it. News timestamps and price timestamps are both in your pack; use them.\n\n**Size.** Is the move unusual for this asset? A 2% move in BTC is ordinary and happens on many days with no news at all. An ordinary move needs no explanation.\n\n**Mechanism.** How would this news change who trades and why? If you cannot describe a path from the item to a change in orders, the link is a guess.\n\nThen list alternatives. A macro release, a large liquidation in derivatives, a move in a related asset, or simple noise could each explain the same move. Your job is to say \"consistent with\" rather than \"caused by\", and to keep the alternatives on the page.\n\n- Timing, size, mechanism: all three, not one.\n- \"Consistent with\" is the strongest honest phrase.\n- Always name at least one alternative explanation.","example":"Suppose BTC is down 3% over 24 hours and the pack contains an article about a regulatory warning, published six hours ago. The move''s timing within those 24 hours is unknown from a snapshot alone. A 3% move is within BTC''s normal range. The mechanism is plausible but vague. Honest wording: ''the fall is consistent with the warning, but the snapshot cannot show timing, the move is within normal range, and a macro or derivatives driver would explain it equally well.''"}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.news.facts-vs-opinions.1","skillId":"news.facts-vs-opinions","prompt":"A headline reads ''Major fund set to buy 1 billion of BTC''. The body says ''a person familiar with the matter said the fund is considering'' the purchase. What is the best summary?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A major fund will buy 1 billion of BTC.","correct":false,"explanation":"This repeats the headline as fact. The body says ''considering'', attributed to an unnamed person. Nothing has been bought or committed.","misconceptionId":"headline-is-fact"},{"id":"b","text":"An outlet reports, citing one unnamed source, that a fund is considering a purchase; unconfirmed.","correct":true,"explanation":"This keeps the attribution, the verb (''considering'') and the sourcing status. It is the only option that matches what the article actually supports."},{"id":"c","text":"Institutional demand for BTC is rising.","correct":false,"explanation":"This turns one unconfirmed report into a narrative about a whole category of buyer. The article contains no data on demand.","misconceptionId":"narrative-is-evidence"},{"id":"d","text":"The report is confirmed because a reputable outlet published it.","correct":false,"explanation":"An outlet publishing a claim from an anonymous source is a lead, not a confirmation. Confirmation needs a document or an independent second source.","misconceptionId":"single-source-certainty"}]},{"id":"kc.news.catalysts.1","skillId":"news.catalysts","prompt":"The macro calendar shows an inflation release tomorrow. Consensus expects 3.1%; the previous reading was 3.4%. Which statement about the catalyst is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The release will move crypto prices because inflation data always does.","correct":false,"explanation":"A catalyst can change expectations, but if the outcome matches the forecast there may be no move. ''Always'' is not supported.","misconceptionId":"catalyst-guarantees-move"},{"id":"b","text":"What matters is the gap between the printed figure and the 3.1% expectation.","correct":true,"explanation":"Prices already reflect the expected 3.1%. A surprise in either direction is what would change expectations; a print at consensus changes little."},{"id":"c","text":"A print of 3.1% would be bullish because it is below 3.4%.","correct":false,"explanation":"The comparison that matters is with the expectation (3.1%), not the previous reading (3.4%). A print at consensus is already priced in.","misconceptionId":"catalyst-guarantees-move"},{"id":"d","text":"Scheduled events are not catalysts because everyone knows they are coming.","correct":false,"explanation":"The date being known does not make the outcome known. Scheduled events are catalysts precisely because the outcome is uncertain."}]},{"id":"kc.news.uncertainty.1","skillId":"news.uncertainty","prompt":"Two news items say an exchange is under investigation. Item one cites ''people familiar with the matter''. Item two links to item one as its source. How many independent sources do you have?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Two, because two outlets published it.","correct":false,"explanation":"Item two relies on item one. Two outlets repeating one report is one source.","misconceptionId":"single-source-certainty"},{"id":"b","text":"One anonymous source; treat the claim as reported and unconfirmed.","correct":true,"explanation":"Independence means the outlets obtained the information separately. Here they did not, and the original source is unnamed."},{"id":"c","text":"None, because anonymous sources never count.","correct":false,"explanation":"An anonymous source is a lead with low confidence, not zero information. The right move is to mark it unconfirmed and look for a document."},{"id":"d","text":"It is confirmed, because an investigation would not be reported unless it were true.","correct":false,"explanation":"Reports from unnamed sources are sometimes wrong or premature. Publication is not confirmation.","misconceptionId":"headline-is-fact"}]},{"id":"kc.news.causation.1","skillId":"news.causation","prompt":"BTC is down 3% over 24 hours. An article about a regulatory warning was published six hours ago. BTC''s usual daily range is 2 to 4%. Which is the most defensible statement?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"BTC fell because of the regulatory warning.","correct":false,"explanation":"You do not know when within the 24 hours the fall happened, the move is within normal range, and other drivers have not been ruled out.","misconceptionId":"correlation-is-causation"},{"id":"b","text":"The fall is consistent with the warning, but timing is unknown, the move is within normal range, and other drivers could explain it.","correct":true,"explanation":"This is the honest form: it checks timing, size and mechanism, and keeps alternative explanations on the page."},{"id":"c","text":"The warning cannot have mattered because 3% is a normal move.","correct":false,"explanation":"A normal-sized move does not rule out a cause; it just means no special explanation is required. You cannot conclude the warning was irrelevant."},{"id":"d","text":"The market is clearly nervous about regulation.","correct":false,"explanation":"''Nervous'' is an interpretation dressed as an observation, and ''clearly'' adds confidence without adding evidence.","misconceptionId":"observation-is-interpretation"}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.news.take-apart-headline","skillIds":["news.facts-vs-opinions","news.uncertainty"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Take a headline apart","difficulty":2,"learningObjective":"Separate facts, attributed claims and opinions in a real news item and rate the sourcing.","evidenceRequirements":["news:market","snapshot:BTC"],"questions":["Pick the news item in the pack that makes the strongest-sounding claim. Write the claim in one sentence, keeping the attribution (''X said that...'').","Which parts of that item are verifiable facts, which are claims by a named or unnamed party, and which are opinion?","Rate the sourcing (primary document, named official, anonymous source, repost). What would raise your confidence?","Does the item say anything that the BTC snapshot can confirm or contradict?"],"expectedReasoning":["Preserves attribution when restating a claim","Classifies sentences as fact, claim or opinion with reasons","Uses publisher, publish time and source link as sourcing evidence","Keeps unconfirmed claims marked as such"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Restates the chosen claim accurately, with the attribution kept","Correctly identifies at least one fact, one claim and one opinion in the item","Correctly classifies the source type","Does not add detail that is not in the item","Reads the snapshot figures correctly if used"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Names the item (title, publisher, time) it is analysing","Quotes or closely paraphrases the sentences it classifies","Uses the item''s metadata (publisher, source link, publish time) in the sourcing rating","Checks the item against the snapshot where a comparison is possible"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why each sentence lands in its category","Explains why the source type leads to the confidence level given","Explains what a second independent source or a document would change","Distinguishes ''published by a reputable outlet'' from ''confirmed''"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Marks the claim as unconfirmed where sourcing is weak","Notes what the item does not say (for example, numbers, dates, documents)","Avoids repeating the headline''s confidence","States what would raise or lower confidence"]}],"keyPoints":["a fact can be checked against a document or dataset","a claim is attributed to a person or organisation and may not be checkable","an opinion is a judgement by the writer or a quoted person","a headline drops attribution and must be checked against the body","a primary document ranks above an unnamed source","two outlets repeating one report is one source","a claim stays unconfirmed until a document or an independent second source appears"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Choosing a different item than the grader expected, as long as the analysis is sound","Treating an on-chain transaction or exchange announcement as a primary document","Describing a research note as opinion with data, provided the data is identified as fact"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["headline-is-fact","single-source-certainty","narrative-is-evidence"]},"hints":["Find the sentence in the body that the headline is based on. Is it longer or more hedged than the headline?","For each sentence ask: could I check this against a record? Is someone named? Is it a judgement word?","A full answer looks like: ''Outlet reports, citing [source type], that [claim]. Facts: [list]. Opinions: [list]. Sourcing: [rating]. Unconfirmed until [what]. The snapshot shows [figure], which neither confirms nor contradicts the claim.''"]},{"id":"ex.news.catalyst-map","skillIds":["news.catalysts","news.narratives"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Map the catalysts and the story","difficulty":2,"learningObjective":"Identify upcoming catalysts from the news and macro feed, state expectations, and test the narrative the coverage is pushing.","evidenceRequirements":["news:macro","news:market","snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH"],"questions":["List the catalysts you can find in the pack. For each, say whether it is scheduled or unscheduled, when it happens, and what outcome seems to be expected.","For the most important catalyst, describe what a surprise would look like in each direction.","What narrative do the market news items share? List the specific facts it rests on, and say which of those facts the pack actually contains.","Which other story would fit the same facts?"],"expectedReasoning":["Distinguishes scheduled from unscheduled catalysts","States expected outcome and surprise conditions rather than predicting","Reduces a narrative to checkable observations","Offers an alternative narrative consistent with the same facts"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Lists catalysts that are actually present in the pack with correct dates","Correctly labels each as scheduled or unscheduled","States the expectation where the pack gives one, and says ''not stated'' where it does not","Names the narrative accurately","Does not assert outcomes as if they had happened"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the macro calendar entries and news items by title and date","Ties each narrative fact to a specific item in the pack","Notes which supporting facts are missing from the pack","Uses snapshot figures only where they bear on the question"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains that the gap between outcome and expectation is what matters","Describes a surprise in both directions, not only the direction the coverage favours","Tests the narrative fact by fact rather than accepting it","Constructs a plausible alternative story from the same facts"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Avoids predicting the outcome of any catalyst","Flags expectations that are the writer''s assumption rather than a stated consensus","Says how strong the narrative''s support is given the facts available","Keeps the possibility that no move follows a catalyst"]}],"keyPoints":["a catalyst is an event that could change expectations","scheduled catalysts have a known date and an unknown outcome","the move depends on outcome minus expectation not the event alone","a narrative is a story that connects facts","each fact behind a narrative must be checked on its own","the same facts can fit more than one narrative","a print at consensus can be a catalyst that changes nothing"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Treating a token unlock, ETF deadline or court date as a scheduled catalyst if it appears in the news items","Saying no clear narrative exists if the items are unrelated, with reasons","Using a range rather than a point for the expected outcome"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["catalyst-guarantees-move","narrative-is-evidence","headline-is-fact"]},"hints":["Look for dates. Anything with a date in the future is a scheduled catalyst. Anything that already happened without warning is unscheduled.","For the narrative: write the story in one sentence, then write the three facts it needs to be true. Check the pack for each.","A full answer lists catalysts with dates and expectations, describes a surprise both ways, names the narrative and its supporting facts, marks which facts the pack lacks, and offers one alternative story."]},{"id":"ex.news.did-the-headline-cause-it","skillIds":["news.causation","news.facts-vs-opinions"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Did the headline cause the move?","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Apply the timing, size and mechanism test to a price move and a news item, and name alternative explanations.","evidenceRequirements":["snapshot:BTC","news:market","news:macro"],"questions":["State BTC''s 24-hour change. Is this move unusual for BTC? Say how you decided.","Pick the news item most likely to be offered as the explanation. Compare its publish time with the snapshot time. What does that tell you about timing?","Describe a mechanism by which that item could change who trades and why. How convincing is it?","List at least two alternative explanations for the same move, and say what evidence would help you choose between them.","Write the strongest honest sentence linking the move to the item."],"expectedReasoning":["Applies timing, size and mechanism as three separate checks","Uses publish and snapshot timestamps as evidence","Judges the size of the move against the asset''s normal range","Keeps alternatives on the page and uses ''consistent with'' language"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["States the 24-hour change correctly","Correctly reads the publish time of the chosen item and the snapshot time","States that a 24-hour change cannot show when within the window the move happened","Describes a mechanism that is at least plausible","Does not assert causation"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the snapshot figure and timestamp","Cites the item''s title, publisher and publish time","Uses the macro feed to find at least one alternative driver","Notes the provenance of each piece of evidence"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Separates timing, size and mechanism and reaches a view on each","Explains why an ordinary-sized move needs no special explanation","Generates alternatives that are specific, not generic","Ends with a ''consistent with'' sentence rather than a causal one"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Names the missing intraday price path as the key gap","Notes that the normal range used is an estimate","Says what evidence would raise confidence in a causal link","Avoids words such as ''clearly'' or ''drove''"]}],"keyPoints":["timing requires knowing which came first to the minute","a snapshot 24-hour change does not show when the move happened","a move inside the asset''s normal range needs no special explanation","a mechanism must describe how the news changes who trades","consistent with is the strongest honest phrase","a macro release or derivatives activity could explain the same move","intraday price data would be needed to test timing"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Choosing a different normal range for BTC if the reasoning is stated","Concluding that no item in the pack plausibly explains the move","Treating a move in ETH or an index as evidence of a market-wide driver"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["correlation-is-causation","headline-is-fact","confidence-is-evidence","observation-is-interpretation"]},"hints":["Three checks, in order: when did each happen, how big was the move, and how would the news change orders?","The snapshot gives you one number for 24 hours. Can it tell you whether the move came before or after the item was published?","A full answer says: the change was X%; this is [within/above] BTC''s usual range; the item was published at T, and the snapshot cannot show when the move happened; mechanism: [path]; alternatives: [macro item, market-wide move]; conclusion: ''consistent with, not shown to be caused by''."]}],"rubricNotes":"Attribution is the core skill here. An answer that restates a headline as fact, or that writes ''because of the news'' without passing the timing, size and mechanism test, should lose most of the ''reasoning'' points even if the facts are right. Reward answers that keep ''reported'' and ''unconfirmed'' in the text and that offer an alternative explanation unprompted.","recommendedNext":"Go on to ''ETF flows'' to apply the same discipline to a dataset that is often reported as news: daily net inflows into spot crypto ETFs."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('etf', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 3, '{"id":"etf","sequence":3,"title":"ETF flows","summary":"Daily net inflow, cumulative net inflow, total net assets and value traded for spot crypto ETFs: what each field measures, how price and flows get mixed up, and why a flow number is not a price-impact number.","learningObjectives":["Read daily net inflow and cumulative net inflow and say what each one measures.","Separate a change in total net assets caused by price from one caused by creations and redemptions.","Explain why flows are reported a day late, why they are a small share of total trading, and why they cannot be read as price impact.","Compare a day''s flow with its own recent history and distinguish one day from a run."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":["markets"],"skillIds":["etf.flows","etf.aum","etf.flow-limits","etf.change-over-time"],"concepts":[{"heading":"What a spot ETF flow is","body":"A spot crypto ETF holds the asset (for example BTC) and issues shares that trade on a stock exchange. When demand for shares exceeds supply, an authorised participant creates new shares by delivering cash or the asset to the fund. When the reverse happens, shares are redeemed. **Net inflow** for a day is the dollar value of creations minus redemptions across the funds counted. A negative number is a net outflow.\n\n**Cumulative net inflow** is the running sum of daily net inflows since the funds launched. It ignores price entirely. It answers \"how much net money has entered the wrapper since the start\", not \"what are the funds worth\" and not \"how much have investors made\".\n\n**Total value traded** is the dollar value of ETF shares that changed hands on the stock exchange that day. Most of that is investors trading shares with each other. It is not a flow. Only the net creations and redemptions are flows.\n\n- Net inflow: creations minus redemptions, in dollars, for one trading day.\n- Cumulative net inflow: the sum of all daily net inflows since launch.\n- Total value traded: share turnover on the exchange, mostly not a flow.","example":"Suppose a day''s aggregate figures read: net inflow +120 million, value traded 2.4 billion, cumulative net inflow 14.0 billion. The honest reading is: on that day, 120 million more came in through creations than left through redemptions; shares worth 2.4 billion changed hands, which is twenty times the net flow; since launch, net money in is 14.0 billion before any price effect."},{"heading":"Total net assets: price and flows, mixed","body":"**Total net assets** (often called assets under management, or AUM) is what the funds hold, valued at today''s price. It changes for two reasons:\n\n- the price of the asset moved, so the same holdings are worth more or less; and\n- shares were created or redeemed, so the holdings themselves grew or shrank.\n\nOnly the second is a flow. On most days, price does more to total net assets than flows do. If the funds hold 50 billion and BTC falls 4%, total net assets fall by about 2 billion with no redemptions at all. A day of 100 million net inflow is a 0.2% effect by comparison.\n\nSo never read a change in total net assets as a flow. Use the reported net inflow for flows, and use the difference between the change in net assets and the net inflow as a rough price effect.\n\nRough decomposition: change in total net assets ≈ net inflow + (previous total net assets × asset return for the day). It will not be exact, because of timing and fees, but it separates the two forces well enough for analysis.","example":"Suppose total net assets went from 50.0 billion to 48.3 billion, and the reported net inflow was +100 million. The 1.7 billion fall cannot be outflows, because the flow was positive. The decomposition gives roughly −1.8 billion from price, which matches a day where the asset fell about 3.6%. Writing ''investors pulled 1.7 billion'' would be wrong."},{"heading":"Three limits of flow data","body":"**Settlement lag.** Creations and redemptions settle after the trading day. Providers publish a day''s flow on the next trading day, and the newest row in a history series may show null for flow fields until it settles. Any flow figure you read describes yesterday or earlier, never right now.\n\n**Scale.** A day''s ETF net flow is usually a small fraction of total spot trading across all venues, and a much smaller fraction once derivatives are included. A 200 million net inflow on a day when spot markets traded 30 billion is under 1% of spot volume.\n\n**No price mapping.** The purchases behind a net inflow are spread across the trading session, often executed by the authorised participant in the way that least moves the market. The price that day was formed by everything that traded, of which ETF demand was one part. So \"inflows pushed the price up X\" and \"outflows caused the fall\" are not conclusions the data supports. Flows tell you about demand for the ETF wrapper. They do not tell you how the price formed.\n\n- Flows are a day late.\n- Flows are small relative to total trading.\n- Flows are a demand measure, not a price-impact measure.","example":"Suppose BTC rose 5% on a day that later shows +300 million net inflow. It is tempting to say the inflow drove the rise. But the flow was unknown until the next day, it was perhaps 1% of that day''s spot volume, and the buying was spread across the session. The defensible sentence: ''ETF demand was positive that day; the price rise was formed by the whole market and cannot be attributed to the inflow.''"},{"heading":"Reading flows over time","body":"Daily flows are noisy. A single positive or negative day tells you about that day. To say anything about a pattern you need a window and a definition.\n\nA simple approach uses the last 10 to 20 trading days from the flow series:\n\n- Count how many days were positive and how many negative.\n- Sum the net inflow over the window.\n- Compare today''s figure with the window''s average and with its largest and smallest values.\n\nThen describe what you found in those terms: \"today''s +150 million is above the 20-day average of +40 million and the largest positive day in that window; 14 of the last 20 days were positive.\" That is a description of a run, with its window stated. It is not a forecast.\n\nThings to avoid: calling one day a trend; comparing a day with a different asset''s flows as if that were a baseline; and reading a slowdown in inflows as outflows. A smaller positive number is still an inflow.\n\nAlso check the dates: the series excludes non-trading days, so a gap in dates is a weekend or holiday, not missing data.","example":"Suppose the last 10 trading days of BTC ETF net inflow were: +80, +120, −40, +60, +200, +90, −10, +150, +70, +110 million. Eight of ten days were positive; the sum is +830 million; the average is +83 million. Today''s +110 million is a little above average and ordinary within the run. You could describe this as a ten-day run of mostly positive flows. You could not say it will continue."}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.etf.flows.1","skillId":"etf.flows","prompt":"A day''s aggregate BTC ETF figures read: net inflow +120 million, total value traded 2.4 billion, cumulative net inflow 14.0 billion. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2.4 billion flowed into the ETFs that day.","correct":false,"explanation":"Value traded is share turnover between investors on the exchange. The flow is the net of creations and redemptions: 120 million."},{"id":"b","text":"Net creations exceeded redemptions by 120 million; shares worth 2.4 billion changed hands; net money in since launch is 14.0 billion before price effects.","correct":true,"explanation":"Each field is read for what it measures. Value traded is not a flow, and cumulative net inflow ignores price."},{"id":"c","text":"Investors have made 14.0 billion in profit since launch.","correct":false,"explanation":"Cumulative net inflow is the sum of money in minus money out. It says nothing about gains; profit would depend on price paid and price now.","misconceptionId":"cumulative-inflow-is-profit"},{"id":"d","text":"The 120 million inflow pushed the price up that day.","correct":false,"explanation":"A flow is a measure of ETF demand. It is small relative to total trading and is settled a day late. It cannot be read as price impact.","misconceptionId":"flows-equal-price-impact"}]},{"id":"kc.etf.aum.1","skillId":"etf.aum","prompt":"Total net assets fell from 50.0 billion to 48.3 billion. Reported net inflow for the day was +100 million. What happened?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Investors withdrew about 1.7 billion.","correct":false,"explanation":"The reported flow was positive, so there was no net withdrawal. The fall in net assets must come from price.","misconceptionId":"aum-change-equals-flows"},{"id":"b","text":"Price fell roughly 3.6%, which outweighed a small positive flow.","correct":true,"explanation":"Change in net assets ≈ flow + price effect. −1.7 billion ≈ +0.1 billion + (−1.8 billion), and 1.8 / 50 ≈ 3.6%."},{"id":"c","text":"The data must be wrong, because net assets cannot fall when inflows are positive.","correct":false,"explanation":"Net assets are holdings valued at today''s price. They fall whenever price falls by more than flows add, which is common.","misconceptionId":"aum-change-equals-flows"},{"id":"d","text":"Cumulative net inflow fell by 1.7 billion.","correct":false,"explanation":"Cumulative net inflow only changes by the day''s net inflow. With a +100 million day it rose by 100 million; price does not touch it.","misconceptionId":"cumulative-inflow-is-profit"}]},{"id":"kc.etf.flow-limits.1","skillId":"etf.flow-limits","prompt":"BTC rose 5% on a day that later showed +300 million of ETF net inflow, on a day when spot markets traded about 30 billion. What is the most defensible statement?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The inflow drove the 5% rise.","correct":false,"explanation":"The flow was about 1% of spot volume, was spread across the session, and was not known until the next day. The data does not support a causal link.","misconceptionId":"flows-equal-price-impact"},{"id":"b","text":"ETF demand was positive that day; the price rise was formed by the whole market and cannot be attributed to the flow.","correct":true,"explanation":"This reads the flow as what it is, a demand measure, and keeps price formation with the market as a whole."},{"id":"c","text":"Flows are irrelevant because they are small.","correct":false,"explanation":"Being small relative to volume limits what flows can explain about price. They still measure ETF demand, which is useful on its own terms."},{"id":"d","text":"Because the flow was known in real time, traders front-ran it and caused the rise.","correct":false,"explanation":"Flows settle after the trading day and are published the next day. They are not known in real time.","misconceptionId":"flows-equal-price-impact"}]},{"id":"kc.etf.change-over-time.1","skillId":"etf.change-over-time","prompt":"The last ten trading days of net inflow were +80, +120, −40, +60, +200, +90, −10, +150, +70, +110 million. Today''s figure is +110 million. Which description is sound?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Inflows are slowing, so outflows have begun.","correct":false,"explanation":"+110 million is an inflow, slightly above the ten-day average. A smaller positive number is not an outflow.","misconceptionId":"one-day-flow-is-trend"},{"id":"b","text":"Eight of the last ten days were positive, the sum is +830 million, and today is a little above the +83 million average.","correct":true,"explanation":"This states the window, the count, the sum and the comparison. It describes a run without forecasting it."},{"id":"c","text":"The trend will continue because most days were positive.","correct":false,"explanation":"A run describes the past window. It does not forecast tomorrow''s flow.","misconceptionId":"one-day-flow-is-trend"},{"id":"d","text":"The −40 million day shows investors turned bearish.","correct":false,"explanation":"A single negative day in a mostly positive window is noise unless you have a reason to think otherwise. One day is not a change of view.","misconceptionId":"one-day-flow-is-trend"}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.etf.read-the-fields","skillIds":["etf.flows","etf.aum"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Read the ETF fields","difficulty":2,"learningObjective":"State what the latest BTC ETF flow point measures and separate the price effect from the flow effect on total net assets.","evidenceRequirements":["etf:BTC","snapshot:BTC"],"questions":["For the latest settled point in the BTC ETF series, state the net inflow, cumulative net inflow, total net assets and total value traded, and say in one sentence what each measures.","Compare the latest total net assets with the previous point. Using the net inflow and the asset''s price change, roughly how much of the change is price and how much is flow?","What is the ratio of net inflow to total value traded on the latest day, and what does that tell you about the share of trading that was a flow?"],"expectedReasoning":["Defines each field correctly","Decomposes the change in net assets into flow and price","Distinguishes value traded from flow","Uses the snapshot''s price change as the price-effect input"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Reads the four fields from the latest settled point correctly","Defines net inflow as creations minus redemptions","Defines cumulative net inflow as the running sum, ignoring price","Defines total net assets as holdings at today''s price","States that value traded is share turnover, mostly not a flow"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Uses the latest settled point, not an unsettled row with null fields","Cites the dates of the points compared","Uses the snapshot''s percentage change as the price input and notes its timestamp","Computes the flow-to-value-traded ratio from the pack''s figures"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Applies the decomposition: change in net assets ≈ flow + price effect","Explains why the price effect usually dominates","Explains why the ratio of flow to value traded is small","Draws only conclusions about demand, not price impact"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Notes that the decomposition is approximate because of timing and fees","Notes that the snapshot''s 24-hour window may not match the ETF trading day","Flags the T+1 settlement lag","Avoids reading any figure as a forecast"]}],"keyPoints":["net inflow is creations minus redemptions for one trading day","cumulative net inflow is the running sum of daily net inflows and ignores price","total net assets is holdings valued at the current price","value traded is share turnover and is mostly not a flow","change in net assets is roughly flow plus price effect","the price effect usually dominates the flow effect","the newest row may be unsettled and show null flow fields"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the ETF series'' own implied price change instead of the snapshot, if explained","Reporting the decomposition as a range rather than a point","Noting that the snapshot and ETF trading day cover different hours"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["aum-change-equals-flows","cumulative-inflow-is-profit","flows-equal-price-impact"]},"hints":["Four fields, four definitions. Which ones are affected by price and which are not?","Net assets change for two reasons. The reported net inflow is one of them. What is left over?","A full answer reads: net inflow X (creations minus redemptions); cumulative Y (sum since launch, no price); net assets Z (holdings at price); value traded W (turnover). Change in Z ≈ X + (previous Z × price return). Flow was about X/W of trading."]},{"id":"ex.etf.flows-and-price","skillIds":["etf.flow-limits","etf.flows"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Flows and price: what you cannot say","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Confront a news item that links flows to price and explain, with the data, why the link cannot be drawn.","evidenceRequirements":["etf:BTC","snapshot:BTC","news:etf"],"questions":["Find a news item in the pack that connects ETF flows to price (or, if none does, describe the kind of sentence you would expect). What exactly does it claim?","Using the latest settled net inflow and the snapshot''s 24-hour volume, estimate the flow as a share of spot trading. What does that number do to the claim?","Explain the settlement lag and what it means for anyone who says flows ''drove'' today''s price.","Write the strongest sentence about flows and price that the data does support."],"expectedReasoning":["Identifies a flows-cause-price claim and restates it precisely","Computes flow as a share of volume","Explains T+1 settlement and its consequence for causal claims","Reframes flows as a demand measure"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Restates the news claim accurately, with attribution","Reads the latest settled net inflow correctly","Reads the snapshot volume correctly and computes the share","States that flows settle and are published after the trading day","Does not assert that flows moved the price"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the news item by title and publisher","Cites the flow date and the snapshot timestamp","Notes that the snapshot volume is spot only and that derivatives would make the share smaller still","Uses the provenance tags to note whether data is live, cached or demo"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why a small share of trading cannot be mapped to a price change","Explains why purchases spread across a session do not map to a single price move","Explains why the lag means the flow was unknown while the price formed","Produces a defensible demand-only statement"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Acknowledges that flows may still be one of many inputs to price","Notes that the volume figure covers only the provider''s venues","Says what data (intraday execution records) would be needed to test the claim","Avoids both ''flows drove it'' and ''flows are irrelevant''"]}],"keyPoints":["a day''s net flow is a small share of spot trading","derivatives trading makes the share smaller still","flows settle after the trading day and are published the next day","the flow was unknown while the price was forming","purchases behind a flow are spread across the session","flows measure demand for the etf wrapper","price was formed by everything that traded"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the ETF series'' own value traded as a denominator alongside spot volume, if both are explained","Concluding that flows and price are ''consistent with each other'' without causal language","Noting that persistent flows over weeks could be a meaningful demand signal even if daily impact is unknowable"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["flows-equal-price-impact","correlation-is-causation","headline-is-fact"]},"hints":["Divide the net inflow by the day''s spot volume. Is the result closer to 50% or to 1%?","When was the flow figure published relative to the price move it is said to explain?","A full answer says: the item claims [X]; flow was about Y% of spot volume; flows settle a day late so were unknown as price formed; purchases are spread across the session; the data supports ''ETF demand was positive'' and nothing about price impact."]},{"id":"ex.etf.run-or-noise","skillIds":["etf.change-over-time","etf.flow-limits"],"kind":"scenario","title":"A run or noise?","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Describe a flow series over a stated window and judge whether the latest day is ordinary within it.","evidenceRequirements":["etf:BTC","etf:ETH","news:etf"],"questions":["For BTC, over the last 10 settled trading days: how many were positive, what is the sum, what is the average, and where does the latest day sit?","Do the same for ETH. Do the two series tell the same story? What can you and can you not conclude from comparing them?","A news item calls the latest day a ''turning point'' or a ''trend''. Using your window, is that description supported?","What would you need to see over the next five trading days to agree that a run had started or ended?"],"expectedReasoning":["Uses a stated window and computes count, sum, average","Places the latest day in context rather than reading it alone","Compares two series without treating one as the baseline for the other","Defines a forward test rather than forecasting"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Counts positive and negative days correctly over the window","Computes sum and average correctly","Places the latest day correctly relative to the average and the window''s range","Reads both series without mixing them up","Does not call a smaller inflow an outflow"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["States the window dates used and excludes unsettled rows","Cites the ETH figures separately from BTC","Quotes the news item''s wording when assessing it","Notes gaps in dates as non-trading days, not missing data"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why a single day cannot be a trend","Explains what ''run'' means with the window stated","Explains why BTC and ETH flows are not baselines for each other","Defines a concrete forward test (for example, sign and size over five days)"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says the description applies to the past window only","Notes that the window choice affects the conclusion","Avoids forecasting the next day","Flags that the series is a day late"]}],"keyPoints":["a window of 10 to 20 trading days is needed to describe a run","count the positive and negative days and sum the flows","compare the latest day with the window average and range","a smaller positive number is still an inflow","one day cannot be a trend","btc and eth flows are separate series and not baselines for each other","a forward test states sign and size over a stated number of days"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using a 15- or 20-day window with the choice stated","Using the median instead of the average","Concluding that the news description is partly supported, with the parts spelled out"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["one-day-flow-is-trend","headline-is-fact","flows-equal-price-impact"]},"hints":["Pick the window first, write the dates, then count. Do not start from today''s number.","A ''turning point'' needs a before and an after. Does your window show both, or just a single day that differs?","A full answer gives count, sum, average and the latest day''s position for each asset, judges the news wording against that, and sets a five-day test with a sign and a size."]}],"rubricNotes":"Mark ''factual'' on correct field definitions and arithmetic. Mark ''reasoning'' heavily on whether the learner keeps flows as a demand measure and refuses to map them to price. Any sentence of the form ''inflows pushed price'' or ''AUM fell so investors sold'' is a critical misconception and should cap the reasoning score. Reward explicit windows and explicit forward tests.","recommendedNext":"Continue to ''Indexes'' to see how weights decide what an aggregate number means, which is the same habit of asking ''what is this number made of'' applied to a basket of assets."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('indexes', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 4, '{"id":"indexes","sequence":4,"title":"Indexes","summary":"What a crypto index is made of: constituents, weights, concentration and diversification, and how to break an index return into member contributions so you never assume the whole basket moved together.","learningObjectives":["Read an index''s constituent list and explain that membership follows rules and is reviewed.","Work out what a weight means for the index return and compare capitalisation-weighted with equal-weighted designs.","Measure concentration as the top-N weight share and explain what it does to risk.","Judge whether an index spreads risk, given how its members tend to move together.","Decompose an index return into weight-times-return contributions and identify which members drove it.","Read an index fund''s NAV per share, relate it to its weighted holdings, and judge how concentrated a themed index is."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":["markets"],"skillIds":["indexes.constituents","indexes.weighting","indexes.concentration","indexes.diversification","indexes.performance","indexes.index-nav"],"concepts":[{"heading":"An index is a rule, applied to a list","body":"A crypto **index** is a number that summarises the price of a basket of assets. The basket is the list of **constituents** (members), and each member has a **weight** that says how much of the index it accounts for. Weights add up to 1 (or 100%).\n\nWho is in the basket is not arbitrary. An index provider publishes a **methodology**: rules such as \"the ten largest assets by market capitalisation that also meet a liquidity threshold, excluding stablecoins\". The list is reviewed on a schedule, say monthly or quarterly, and assets enter or leave when they cross the rules. So a member today may not be a member next quarter.\n\nThe index also has a **value** (a level, like 20.93) that is only meaningful relative to its own past, and a **24-hour change** and longer returns (7-day, 1-month, year-to-date) that are usually quoted as fractions or percentages.\n\nWhen you read an index snapshot, start with three questions:\n\n- What is the selection rule, and when was the list last reviewed?\n- What are the weights, from largest to smallest?\n- Which returns are shown, and over which windows?","example":"Suppose an index called ''Layer 1'' lists seven constituents with BTC at 31%, ETH at 24%, and five others sharing the remaining 45%. The index value is 20.93 and its 24-hour change is −0.16%. The level 20.93 means nothing on its own; the weights and the rule that produced them are where the information is."},{"heading":"Weights decide what moves the index","body":"The index return over a period is the sum, across members, of each member''s weight multiplied by that member''s return. That is the single formula you need:\n\nindex return ≈ Σ (weight × member return)\n\nA member with a 40% weight that rises 5% adds 2 percentage points to the index. A member with a 2% weight that rises 50% adds 1 point. The big weight matters more than the big move.\n\nMost crypto indexes are **capitalisation-weighted**: weight is proportional to market cap, sometimes with a cap so one asset cannot exceed, say, 40%. The alternative is **equal weight**, where every member has the same weight regardless of size. The two designs give different returns from the same members. Equal weight gives small members as much say as large ones, so it is more sensitive to the smaller, usually more volatile, names.\n\nUnless the methodology says equal weight, assume it is not. \"One of seven members\" does not mean \"one seventh of the index\".\n\n- Weight × return = contribution.\n- Capitalisation weight: big assets dominate.\n- Equal weight: small assets matter as much as big ones.","example":"Suppose a two-member index has A at 80% and B at 20%. Over a day, A falls 1% and B rises 10%. Index return ≈ 0.8 × (−1%) + 0.2 × 10% = −0.8% + 2.0% = +1.2%. An equal-weight version would return 0.5 × (−1%) + 0.5 × 10% = +4.5%. Same members, different number."},{"heading":"Concentration: how much sits at the top","body":"**Concentration** describes how much of the index is held by its largest members. A simple measure is the **top-N weight share**: add the weights of the biggest N members. If the top two hold 70%, the index is highly concentrated even if it has twenty names.\n\nConcentration is not a flaw; it is a property. It means the index behaves mostly like its largest members. A 20-name index with 70% in two assets will track those two assets closely, and the other eighteen will barely move the figure. The number of names tells you little. The weight distribution tells you almost everything.\n\nWhy it matters for risk: if one heavily weighted member has an asset-specific problem (a protocol bug, a delisting), the index takes most of that hit. In a less concentrated index, the same event would be diluted.\n\nA quick reading routine:\n\n- Sort weights descending.\n- Report the top-1, top-3 and top-5 shares.\n- Say which members, if they moved alone, could move the index by a full percentage point.","example":"Suppose an index has ten members with weights 35%, 25%, 12%, 8%, 6%, 5%, 4%, 3%, 1%, 1%. The top-2 share is 60%; the top-5 share is 86%. The bottom five together hold 14%. If the largest member fell 3% and everything else was flat, the index would fall about 1.05%. If the smallest member fell 50%, the index would fall 0.5%."},{"heading":"Diversification depends on co-movement, not count","body":"**Diversification** is the reduction of risk you get from holding things that do not all move together. Holding many names helps only if their returns are at least partly independent.\n\nCrypto assets tend to move together, especially in stress. When BTC falls sharply, most other crypto assets usually fall too, often by more. So an index of ten crypto assets diversifies away some **asset-specific** risk (one protocol failing) but little of the **market-wide** risk (the whole sector falling). It is spread, but spread across things that mostly rise and fall as a group.\n\nConcentration makes this worse: a concentrated index gets both high exposure to one name and high exposure to the sector.\n\nHonest language for an index: \"spreads single-asset risk across N names with top-3 share X%; members tend to move together, so it does not protect against a sector-wide fall.\" Avoid \"diversified\" as a bare adjective.\n\n- Count of names is not diversification.\n- Co-movement limits diversification, and crypto co-movement is high.\n- Say which risk the index spreads and which it does not.","example":"Suppose an index of seven large-cap assets fell 8% on a day when each member fell between 6% and 11%. Seven names did not protect it, because they all moved the same way. The index did protect against the case where one member alone fell 40%: that member''s weight would have limited the damage."},{"heading":"Decomposing an index return","body":"When an index moves, do not assume every member moved the same way. The index is a weighted sum, so one heavy member rising can lift the index while most members fall.\n\nTo decompose a return you need each member''s weight and each member''s return over the same window. Multiply them, list the contributions, and sort. Then you can say which members drove the result and which worked against it. The sum of the contributions should be close to the index return (rounding and intra-period weight drift explain small differences).\n\nIf you only have member weights and the index return, you cannot do the full decomposition. You can still bound things: if the index rose 2% and the largest member (40%) was flat, the other 60% must have contributed the full 2%, implying an average member return around 3.3% among them.\n\nGuard against the error in both directions: \"the index is up so the sector is up\" and \"the sector is up so every coin is up\". Always check the spread of member returns.\n\n- Contribution = weight × member return.\n- Sort contributions to find the drivers.\n- Report how many members rose and how many fell, not only the index figure.","example":"Suppose an index rose 2.0% with weights A 50%, B 30%, C 20% and returns A +6%, B −2%, C −3%. Contributions: A +3.0, B −0.6, C −0.6, total +1.8% (close to 2.0% after rounding). Two of three members fell, yet the index rose. ''The sector rallied'' would misdescribe this day."},{"heading":"Index NAV and themed indexes","body":"An **index fund** turns an index into something you can hold. It owns the index''s members in their weights, and divides the total value by the number of shares. That figure is the **net asset value (NAV) per share**.\n\nNAV moves for one reason: the value of the holdings changes. If a fund holds four tokens at weights 40%, 30%, 20% and 10%, and the 40% token rises 10% while the rest are flat, NAV rises about 4%. Weights drift as prices move, so funds **rebalance** on a schedule back to the target weights.\n\nTwo things to check on any index fund:\n\n- **The theme.** A broad index picks members by size across a whole market. A **themed index** picks members that share one story, such as one ecosystem or one sector. Themed members tend to rise and fall together, so a themed index spreads single-token risk but not the risk of the theme falling out of favour.\n- **The weights behind the NAV.** If the top two members hold 60%, NAV mostly follows those two.\n\nNAV is also stamped with a time. Compare it with the traded price of the fund''s share only when both carry similar times.\n\nPlain description: \"NAV X per share as of <time>; themed on Y; top-2 weight Z%.\"","example":"Suppose a themed index fund has NAV 10.00 per share and holds five tokens from one ecosystem at 35%, 25%, 20%, 12% and 8%. If the whole ecosystem falls 20% in a week, NAV falls about 20% too: holding five names did not help, because they share one theme. If only the 8% member fell 50%, NAV would fall about 4%."}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.indexes.weighting.1","skillId":"indexes.weighting","prompt":"A two-member index has A at 80% and B at 20%. Over a day, A falls 1% and B rises 10%. What is the index return, roughly?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"+4.5%, the average of the two returns.","correct":false,"explanation":"That is the equal-weight answer. This index is 80/20, so A''s return counts four times as much as B''s.","misconceptionId":"index-is-equal-weight"},{"id":"b","text":"+1.2%: 0.8 × (−1%) + 0.2 × 10%.","correct":true,"explanation":"Index return is the sum of weight times member return. −0.8% + 2.0% = +1.2%."},{"id":"c","text":"+10%, because B rose 10%.","correct":false,"explanation":"B is only a fifth of the index. Its 10% rise contributes 2 percentage points, not 10.","misconceptionId":"index-return-is-every-asset"},{"id":"d","text":"−1%, because A is the larger member.","correct":false,"explanation":"A dominates, but B''s contribution is not zero. 0.2 × 10% = +2% outweighs A''s −0.8%."}]},{"id":"kc.indexes.concentration.1","skillId":"indexes.concentration","prompt":"An index has ten members with weights 35, 25, 12, 8, 6, 5, 4, 3, 1, 1 (percent). Which statement is most accurate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is well diversified because it has ten members.","correct":false,"explanation":"The count of names says little. The top two hold 60% and the top five hold 86%, so the index behaves mostly like its largest members.","misconceptionId":"concentration-is-diversification"},{"id":"b","text":"Top-2 share is 60% and top-5 share is 86%; the bottom five barely move the index.","correct":true,"explanation":"Reporting top-N shares is the right way to describe concentration. The bottom five hold 14% combined."},{"id":"c","text":"Each member contributes 10% of the index return.","correct":false,"explanation":"Weights are given and they are not equal. Contribution is weight times return, so the 35% member matters 35 times as much as a 1% member.","misconceptionId":"index-is-equal-weight"},{"id":"d","text":"The smallest member is the riskiest for the index.","correct":false,"explanation":"Even a 50% fall in a 1% member moves the index 0.5%. A 3% fall in the 35% member moves it about 1.05%. Weight sets the index-level risk."}]},{"id":"kc.indexes.performance.1","skillId":"indexes.performance","prompt":"An index rose 2% with weights A 50%, B 30%, C 20% and member returns A +6%, B −2%, C −3%. Which description of the day is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The sector rallied; all members gained.","correct":false,"explanation":"Two of three members fell. A''s +6% on a 50% weight (+3 points) outweighed B and C''s combined −1.2 points.","misconceptionId":"index-return-is-every-asset"},{"id":"b","text":"A contributed about +3 points; B and C together about −1.2; two of three members fell.","correct":true,"explanation":"Contribution = weight × return. Reporting the drivers and the count of risers and fallers is the honest description."},{"id":"c","text":"The index must be wrong, because most members fell.","correct":false,"explanation":"A weighted sum can rise while most members fall, when a heavy member rises enough. Nothing is wrong with the figure.","misconceptionId":"index-is-equal-weight"},{"id":"d","text":"Each member rose by about 2%.","correct":false,"explanation":"The index return is not the return of each member. Member returns here range from −3% to +6%.","misconceptionId":"index-return-is-every-asset"}]},{"id":"kc.indexes.diversification.1","skillId":"indexes.diversification","prompt":"An index of seven large-cap crypto assets fell 8% on a day when every member fell between 6% and 11%. What does this show about diversification?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The index is not diversified, so it is badly built.","correct":false,"explanation":"The index did spread single-asset risk across seven names. What it could not do is protect against a sector-wide fall, because members moved together."},{"id":"b","text":"Diversification across assets that move together reduces single-asset risk but not sector-wide risk.","correct":true,"explanation":"This names which risk was spread and which was not. Co-movement, not the count of names, limits the benefit."},{"id":"c","text":"With seven members the index should have been protected from an 8% fall.","correct":false,"explanation":"Seven names only help if their returns are partly independent. Crypto assets usually fall together in stress.","misconceptionId":"diversification-removes-risk"},{"id":"d","text":"The index is diversified because it has seven constituents.","correct":false,"explanation":"Count is not diversification. The day''s returns show the members moved as a group, which is exactly what diversification cannot fix.","misconceptionId":"concentration-is-diversification"}]},{"id":"kc.indexes.index-nav.1","skillId":"indexes.index-nav","prompt":"A themed index fund holds five tokens from one ecosystem at weights 35, 25, 20, 12 and 8 percent. Its NAV per share is 10.00. Which statement is most accurate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is a broad index, so a fall in that ecosystem would barely move NAV.","correct":false,"explanation":"All five members share one theme, so an ecosystem-wide fall moves nearly all of the NAV.","misconceptionId":"themed-index-is-broad"},{"id":"b","text":"NAV mostly follows the top two members (60%), and a theme-wide fall would hit almost all of it.","correct":true,"explanation":"Weights set what drives NAV, and a shared theme means the members tend to move together."},{"id":"c","text":"Each member moves NAV by the same amount, because there are five of them.","correct":false,"explanation":"Weights are not equal. A 10% move in the 35% member changes NAV by about 3.5%; the same move in the 8% member changes it by 0.8%.","misconceptionId":"index-is-equal-weight"},{"id":"d","text":"A NAV of 10.00 means the fund is cheap.","correct":false,"explanation":"NAV per share depends on how many shares exist. On its own it says nothing about value or cheapness.","misconceptionId":"price-equals-value"}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.indexes.read-the-basket","skillIds":["indexes.constituents","indexes.weighting","indexes.concentration"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Read the basket","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Describe an index from its constituent list and weights: who is in it, how concentrated it is, and which members could move it by a full percentage point.","evidenceRequirements":["index:all","snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH"],"questions":["Pick one index from the pack. List its constituents in weight order and report the top-1, top-3 and top-5 weight shares.","Is the index capitalisation-weighted, equal-weighted, or something else? What in the data supports your answer, and what would you need to confirm it?","Which members, moving alone by 3%, would move the index by at least 1 percentage point?","What does the constituent list not tell you about how the index is built?"],"expectedReasoning":["Sorts and sums weights correctly","Infers the weighting scheme from the weight distribution and the snapshots","Applies weight × return to find the members that matter","Names the methodology (selection rules, review schedule) as missing"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Lists constituents and weights correctly from the index snapshot","Computes top-1, top-3 and top-5 shares correctly","Correctly identifies which members at 3% would move the index by 1 point (weight ≥ about 33%)","Does not assume equal weight unless the weights are equal","Describes the index level as meaningful only relative to its own history"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the index name and as-of time","Uses the BTC and ETH snapshots (market cap) to test the capitalisation-weight hypothesis","Quotes the weights as given (fractions or percentages) without rounding errors that change the ranking","Notes the provenance of the index data"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why top-N share is a better concentration measure than member count","Explains the link between weight and index-level sensitivity","Reasons from the ratio of BTC to ETH weights versus their market caps to a weighting hypothesis","Identifies the methodology as the thing that would confirm or refute the hypothesis"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Marks the weighting scheme as inferred, not confirmed","Notes that weights drift between rebalances","Names the missing information: selection rule, review schedule, caps","Avoids calling the index diversified or concentrated without a number"]}],"keyPoints":["constituents are chosen by a published rule and reviewed on a schedule","weights add up to one and are listed descending","top-n weight share measures concentration","contribution equals weight multiplied by member return","a member needs a weight of about a third to move the index one point on a 3% move","the ratio of weights to market caps suggests the weighting scheme","the methodology is not in the pack and must be treated as unknown"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Reporting top-2 instead of top-3 if the index has few members","Concluding ''capped capitalisation weight'' if the largest weight looks capped","Using a 2% or 5% hypothetical move with the threshold recomputed"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["index-is-equal-weight","concentration-is-diversification","index-members-are-fixed"]},"hints":["Sort the weights from largest to smallest and add from the top. The first three numbers you write are top-1, top-3, top-5.","If BTC''s market cap is about three times ETH''s, what ratio of weights would you expect under capitalisation weighting? Compare with the pack.","A full answer gives the sorted list, the three shares, a weighting hypothesis tied to the market cap ratio, the members above the one-third threshold, and says the selection rule and review date are not in the pack."]},{"id":"ex.indexes.who-moved-it","skillIds":["indexes.performance","indexes.weighting"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Who moved the index?","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Use weights and whatever member returns are available to say which constituents drove an index''s 24-hour change, and what you cannot decompose.","evidenceRequirements":["index:all","snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH"],"questions":["Pick one index. State its 24-hour change. For the members whose 24-hour returns you have (from the snapshots), compute each one''s contribution (weight × return).","What do those contributions add up to, and what does the gap between that sum and the index change tell you about the members you cannot see?","Can you say whether ''most members rose'' or ''most members fell''? If not, what would you need?","Write the one-sentence description of the day that the data supports, and one that it does not."],"expectedReasoning":["Computes contributions for known members","Bounds the contribution of unknown members from the residual","Refuses to describe the breadth of the move without member returns","Writes observation and interpretation as separate sentences"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["States the index 24-hour change correctly","Computes contributions for BTC and ETH correctly using the index weights and snapshot returns","Computes the residual correctly","Infers the average return of the unseen members from the residual and their combined weight","Does not claim every member moved the same way"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the index, its weights and its as-of time","Cites the snapshot returns and their timestamps","Notes that the index window and the snapshot windows may not line up exactly","Uses only members present in both the index and the snapshots for the computation"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains the contribution formula and applies it","Explains how the residual bounds the unseen members","Explains why breadth (how many rose or fell) cannot be read from the index figure","Keeps the supported sentence to observations and the unsupported one clearly labelled"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Flags window mismatch between sources as a reason the sum may not match","Says that weights may have drifted since the last rebalance","Names member-level returns as the missing data for a full decomposition","Avoids ''the sector rallied'' or ''the sector sold off'' language"]}],"keyPoints":["contribution equals weight multiplied by member return","sum the known contributions and compare with the index change","the residual is the combined contribution of unseen members","the residual divided by the unseen weight gives their average return","breadth cannot be read from the index figure alone","the index and snapshot windows may differ","member-level returns are needed for a full decomposition"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Expressing contributions in percentage points or in index points, if consistent","Choosing a different index from the pack","Stating a range for the unseen members'' average return given window mismatch"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["index-return-is-every-asset","index-is-equal-weight","observation-is-interpretation"]},"hints":["You know two members'' returns and all the weights. Multiply, add, and compare with the index change.","Whatever is left over came from the members you cannot see. Divide it by their combined weight to get their average move.","A full answer gives BTC and ETH contributions, the residual, the implied average return of the rest, says breadth is unknown without member returns, and writes a supported sentence like ''the index fell X%, with BTC contributing Y points'' and flags ''the sector fell'' as unsupported."]},{"id":"ex.indexes.is-it-diversified","skillIds":["indexes.diversification","indexes.concentration"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Is it diversified?","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Judge an index''s diversification from its weights and the observed co-movement of its members, and say which risk it spreads and which it does not.","evidenceRequirements":["index:all","snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH","news:market"],"questions":["Compare two indexes from the pack (or one index with an imagined equal-weight version of itself). Which is more concentrated, by top-3 share?","Using the BTC and ETH 24-hour changes and the index 24-hour changes, what can you say about whether members moved together today? What can you not say from one day?","For the more concentrated index, name one asset-specific event that would hurt it badly and one sector-wide event that neither index would be protected from.","Write a one-sentence honest description of each index''s diversification that includes a number."],"expectedReasoning":["Measures concentration numerically before comparing","Uses co-movement, not count, as the basis for diversification claims","Separates asset-specific from market-wide risk","Qualifies any co-movement claim made from a single day"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Computes top-3 shares correctly for both indexes","Reads the 24-hour changes correctly for BTC, ETH and each index","States that diversification depends on co-movement rather than member count","Distinguishes asset-specific from sector-wide risk correctly","Does not describe either index as ''safe'' because it is diversified"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the weights and as-of times of both indexes","Uses the snapshot changes as evidence of same-day co-movement","Uses a news item, if relevant, as an example of an asset-specific or sector-wide event","Notes which members'' returns are not in the pack"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why top-3 share, not count, measures concentration","Explains how one day of same-sign moves is weak evidence of co-movement, and what longer data would show","Reasons from weights to the damage an asset-specific event would do","Produces descriptions that name the risk spread and the risk not spread"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Marks the co-movement claim as based on one day and therefore weak","Says that historical correlation data is missing","Avoids implying that diversification protects against a sector fall","Keeps the event examples hypothetical"]}],"keyPoints":["top-3 weight share is a concentration measure","diversification depends on how members move together","crypto assets tend to fall together in stress","an index spreads single-asset risk but not sector-wide risk","one day of same-sign moves is weak evidence of co-movement","a heavy member with a specific problem hurts a concentrated index most","historical correlation data is needed to judge co-movement properly"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using top-2 or top-5 share with the choice stated","Constructing the equal-weight comparison if only one index is in the pack","Using the index''s 7-day or 1-month returns alongside the 24-hour change if available"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["concentration-is-diversification","diversification-removes-risk","index-is-equal-weight"]},"hints":["Numbers first: top-3 share for each. Then ask whether the members moved the same way today.","Think of two kinds of bad day: one coin has a problem, or the whole sector falls. Which one does spreading across coins help with?","A full answer compares top-3 shares, uses today''s changes as weak evidence of co-movement, gives one asset-specific and one sector-wide event, and writes sentences like ''spreads single-asset risk across N names with top-3 share X%; members move together, so a sector fall would hit it in full''."]}],"rubricNotes":"Arithmetic matters here: contributions, top-N shares and residuals should be correct to rounding. Reasoning points depend on the learner refusing two errors: treating members as equal-weighted without evidence, and reading the index return as the return of each member. Reward answers that name the methodology as missing and that say which risk an index spreads and which it does not.","recommendedNext":"Next is ''The order book'', where you will look inside a single venue at the resting orders that produce the prices you have been reading."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('orderbook', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 5, '{"id":"orderbook","sequence":5,"title":"The order book","summary":"Inside a limit order book: bids and asks, the spread, depth, liquidity, imbalance, tick and lot size, and price-time priority. Read a book as a snapshot of resting orders and estimate what a trade would cost, without turning the picture into a forecast.","learningObjectives":["Read best bid, best ask and mid price from a book and say which price a market buyer and a market seller get.","Compute the spread in ticks and as a percentage of mid, and explain it as the cost of immediacy, separate from exchange fees.","Read per-level and cumulative depth, and estimate the average price and the worst price a given order size would reach.","Judge liquidity from spread and depth within a fixed distance of mid, and explain why total book size is not the right measure.","Apply tick size, lot size, minimum quantity and minimum notional to a proposed order, and work out fill order under price-time priority.","Compare the spot and perpetual books for the same asset on spread and depth, and explain why the tighter book is not the cheaper one for every order size."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":["markets"],"skillIds":["orderbook.bid-ask","orderbook.spread","orderbook.depth","orderbook.liquidity","orderbook.imbalance","orderbook.tick-lot","orderbook.price-time-priority","orderbook.spot-vs-perps"],"concepts":[{"heading":"Bids, asks and the mid","body":"A **limit order book** is the list of orders that are waiting to trade on one venue. **Bids** are offers to buy at a stated price or lower. **Asks** are offers to sell at a stated price or higher. Each side is grouped by price into **levels**, and each level shows the total quantity resting there. Bids are listed from highest price down; asks from lowest price up.\n\nThe **best bid** is the highest bid. The **best ask** is the lowest ask. The **mid price** is halfway between them. Nobody is offering to trade at the mid. It is a reference number.\n\nWho pays what:\n\n- A **market buy** takes the best ask, then the next ask, and so on until it is filled. The buyer pays the ask side.\n- A **market sell** hits the best bid, then the next bid. The seller receives the bid side.\n- A **limit order** that does not cross the spread rests in the book and waits.\n\nAn order that takes resting liquidity is a **taker**. An order that rests is a **maker**. Venues often charge takers more than makers. The book is a snapshot at one block time: orders can be added or cancelled at any moment.","example":"Suppose a BTC/USDC book shows best bid 59,990 for 0.4 and best ask 60,000 for 0.3. The mid is 59,995. A market buy of 0.1 BTC pays 60,000. A market sell of 0.1 BTC receives 59,990. Nobody can buy or sell at 59,995 right now; a limit order placed there would rest between the two sides."},{"heading":"The spread is a cost, not a fee","body":"The **spread** is best ask minus best bid. Express it two ways: in **ticks** (how many price steps wide) and as a **percentage of mid** (spread ÷ mid × 100), which lets you compare markets at different price levels.\n\nThe spread is the cost of trading immediately. If you buy at the ask and sell straight back at the bid, you lose the spread. Who gets it? The makers whose resting orders you traded against, not the exchange.\n\nThe **exchange fee** is a separate charge, usually a small percentage of the trade value, with different rates for makers and takers. It is added on top of the spread. The two are easy to confuse because both are costs of trading, but they go to different parties and behave differently: the spread changes from second to second with the book; the fee is a fixed schedule.\n\nA rough all-in cost for a small round trip: spread percentage + 2 × taker fee percentage.\n\n- Spread in ticks: how wide, in price steps.\n- Spread as % of mid: comparable across markets.\n- Fee: a separate charge set by the venue, not the spread.","example":"Suppose best bid 59,999 and best ask 60,000 on a market with tick size 1 and taker fee 0.065%. The spread is 1 tick, or about 0.0017% of mid. A round trip of a small size costs roughly 0.0017% + 2 × 0.065% ≈ 0.13%. The fee dominates here; on a wide-spread market the spread would."},{"heading":"Depth: how much, and how far","body":"**Depth** is the quantity resting at each level, and **cumulative depth** is the running total from the top of the book outwards. Depth tells you what a given order size would do.\n\nTo estimate the fill of a market order:\n\n- Walk down the levels on the side you trade against, taking each level''s quantity until the order is filled.\n- The last level you touch is the **worst price** you would pay or receive.\n- The average of the prices you took, weighted by quantity, is the **average fill price**.\n- The difference between the average fill and the mid (or the best price) is the **price impact**, usually shown as a percentage.\n\nDepth is a snapshot. The orders you count can be cancelled before your order arrives, and new ones can appear. So any estimate carries the caveat \"if the book stays as it was at this block time\".\n\nCompare depth within a fixed distance of mid, for example within 0.5%, across markets or across time. Total depth across all levels is dominated by far-away orders that a normal-sized trade never touches.","example":"Suppose the asks are 60,000 for 0.3, 60,001 for 0.5, 60,002 for 1.0. A market buy of 1.0 BTC takes 0.3 at 60,000, 0.5 at 60,001 and 0.2 at 60,002. Worst price 60,002. Average ≈ (0.3 × 60,000 + 0.5 × 60,001 + 0.2 × 60,002) / 1.0 = 60,000.9. Impact over best ask about 0.0015%. The same order on a book with 0.05 per level would walk much further."},{"heading":"Liquidity and imbalance","body":"**Liquidity** in a book is spread and depth together: a narrow spread with plenty of quantity close to the mid means a trade of ordinary size is cheap. A book can have a large total quantity and still be illiquid, if most of that quantity sits far from the mid. So compare books by spread and by depth within a fixed band, not by total size.\n\n**Imbalance** compares the two sides. One measure is **bid share of depth**: bid quantity ÷ (bid quantity + ask quantity) over a fixed number of levels. A value of 0.5 is balanced; 0.7 means bids hold 70% of the counted depth.\n\nWhat imbalance is: a description of resting orders at one moment. What it is not: a forecast. Resting orders can be cancelled in milliseconds, can be placed to give an impression and then pulled, and only matter if an incoming order trades against them. A large resting bid is a large resting bid. It is not \"support\" and it is not \"buying pressure\".\n\n- Liquidity: spread and depth near mid, not total size.\n- Imbalance: bid share of depth over stated levels.\n- Both are snapshots. Neither forecasts the next trade.","example":"Suppose two books both hold 50 BTC in total. Book A has 10 BTC within 0.5% of mid and a 1-tick spread. Book B has 1 BTC within 0.5% and the rest more than 3% away, with a 20-tick spread. Book A is more liquid for any ordinary order, although both books are the same size. If Book A shows bid share 0.72 over ten levels, that is a description of the book now; whether price moves up or down next is a separate question that the number does not answer."},{"heading":"Tick, lot and priority: the matching rules","body":"A venue sets a grid. **Tick size** is the smallest price step: prices must be integer multiples of it. **Lot size** (often called step size) is the smallest quantity step: sizes must be integer multiples of it. These are two separate rules, one for price and one for quantity.\n\nThere are also size limits that are not the same as the grid: **minimum quantity** (the smallest order allowed) and **minimum notional** (the smallest price × quantity allowed). An order must satisfy all four at once. A quantity can be a valid multiple of the lot size and still be below the minimum notional.\n\nOnce orders are in the book, the venue matches them by **price-time priority**: the best price fills first, and at the same price the order that arrived first fills first. Size does not give priority. A large order at the same price as an earlier small one waits behind it. When an incoming order is bigger than the first resting order, it takes that order in full, then moves to the next in time order, then to the next price level.\n\n- Tick size: price grid. Lot size: quantity grid.\n- Minimum quantity and minimum notional: separate size floors.\n- Price first, then time. Never size.","example":"Suppose tick size 1, lot size 0.00001, minimum quantity 0.00001 and minimum notional 5 USDC on BTC/USDC near 60,000. A buy at 60,000.5 is rejected (not a tick multiple). A buy of 0.00005 at 60,000 is a valid lot multiple but has notional 3 USDC, below the minimum, so it is rejected. At the best bid, order X (0.2, placed 10:00:01) and order Y (1.0, placed 10:00:03) rest. A market sell of 0.5 fills X in full (0.2) and then 0.3 of Y. X went first because it was earlier, not because of size."},{"heading":"Same asset, two books: spot and perpetual","body":"One asset can trade on more than one book on the same venue. The **spot** book exchanges the token itself for a quote currency. A **perpetual** (perp) book trades a contract that tracks the asset''s price, with no expiry. They are separate markets with their own resting orders, so they have their own best bid, best ask, spread and depth.\n\nThe perp''s price is kept close to spot by a **funding** payment that passes between long and short holders at intervals. Funding is a real cost or income for perp positions, but it is not shown on an order book, so a book-only comparison leaves it out. Say so when you compare.\n\nWhy can a perp book be tighter? Derivative traders and market makers often concentrate there, and quoting a contract needs less capital than holding the token. Why can spot still be better for some orders? A tight perp spread may rest on small sizes at the top, while spot has fewer, larger orders a few ticks away.\n\nTo compare fairly for the same asset:\n\n- Spread as a percentage of mid for each book.\n- Depth in **quote value** (price × quantity) over the same number of levels.\n- The average price a stated order size would get on each.\n\nNeither book alone is \"the\" price of the asset.","example":"Suppose spot shows 99.90 / 100.10 with 30 tokens on each of the top levels, and the perp shows 99.99 / 100.01 with 2 contracts per level. The perp''s spread is 0.02% against spot''s 0.20%. A buyer of 1 unit pays less on the perp. A buyer of 60 units walks the perp book many levels and may pay more than on spot, where 60 units fill within two levels."}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.orderbook.bid-ask.1","skillId":"orderbook.bid-ask","prompt":"A BTC/USDC book shows best bid 59,990 for 0.4 and best ask 60,000 for 0.3. A trader sends a market buy for 0.1 BTC. What price do they pay?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"59,995, the mid price.","correct":false,"explanation":"Nobody is offering to sell at the mid. It is the average of the best bid and best ask, not a quote anyone has posted.","misconceptionId":"mid-price-is-tradeable"},{"id":"b","text":"60,000, the best ask.","correct":true,"explanation":"A market buy takes the lowest resting ask. 0.1 is less than the 0.3 available there, so the whole order fills at 60,000."},{"id":"c","text":"59,990, the best bid.","correct":false,"explanation":"The best bid is what a market seller receives. A buyer trades against the ask side."},{"id":"d","text":"It depends on how many bids there are.","correct":false,"explanation":"Bid quantity is irrelevant to a buy order. The buyer consumes asks, and the fill depends only on the ask side.","misconceptionId":"imbalance-predicts-price"}]},{"id":"kc.orderbook.spread.1","skillId":"orderbook.spread","prompt":"Best bid 59,999, best ask 60,000, tick size 1, taker fee 0.065%. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The spread of 1 tick is the fee the exchange charges per trade.","correct":false,"explanation":"The spread goes to the maker whose order you trade against. The exchange fee (0.065% here) is a separate charge on top.","misconceptionId":"spread-is-fee"},{"id":"b","text":"The spread is 1 tick (about 0.0017% of mid); a small round trip costs roughly spread plus two taker fees, about 0.13%.","correct":true,"explanation":"Spread and fee are both costs but go to different parties. On this tight book, the fee is the larger part of the all-in cost."},{"id":"c","text":"Because the spread is 1 tick, trading here is free apart from the spread.","correct":false,"explanation":"The taker fee applies to every taking trade regardless of how narrow the spread is.","misconceptionId":"spread-is-fee"},{"id":"d","text":"The spread cannot be expressed as a percentage because it is quoted in ticks.","correct":false,"explanation":"Spread ÷ mid × 100 gives the percentage. 1 ÷ 59,999.5 × 100 ≈ 0.0017%."}]},{"id":"kc.orderbook.depth.1","skillId":"orderbook.depth","prompt":"Asks are 60,000 for 0.3, 60,001 for 0.5, 60,002 for 1.0. A market buy of 1.0 BTC arrives. What is the worst price paid, and what caveat applies?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"60,000; the whole order fills at the best ask.","correct":false,"explanation":"Only 0.3 is available at 60,000. The order must walk to 60,001 (0.5) and then 60,002 (0.2)."},{"id":"b","text":"60,002; and the book may have changed before the order arrived.","correct":true,"explanation":"0.3 + 0.5 + 0.2 = 1.0, so the last level touched is 60,002. Any estimate assumes the book stayed as it was at the snapshot''s block time."},{"id":"c","text":"60,002; and this is guaranteed because the orders are resting.","correct":false,"explanation":"Resting orders can be cancelled or changed at any time. A snapshot describes the book at one block, not at the moment your order lands.","misconceptionId":"depth-is-static"},{"id":"d","text":"60,001, the average of the levels touched.","correct":false,"explanation":"The average fill is about 60,000.9, but the question asks for the worst price, which is the last level touched: 60,002."}]},{"id":"kc.orderbook.imbalance.1","skillId":"orderbook.imbalance","prompt":"Over the top ten levels, bids total 7.2 BTC and asks total 2.8 BTC, giving bid share of depth 0.72. What does this tell you?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Price is likely to rise because buyers outweigh sellers.","correct":false,"explanation":"Resting bids are not buyers who have traded. They can be cancelled instantly and only matter if an order trades into them. Imbalance describes the book; it does not forecast.","misconceptionId":"imbalance-predicts-price"},{"id":"b","text":"At this block time, 72% of the counted depth sat on the bid side; nothing about the next trade follows.","correct":true,"explanation":"This is the full content of the number: a description of resting orders over a stated number of levels at one moment."},{"id":"c","text":"There is strong support at the bid, so a fall is unlikely.","correct":false,"explanation":"''Support'' is an interpretation. A large resting bid can be pulled in milliseconds, and the book cannot tell you whether it will be.","misconceptionId":"imbalance-predicts-price"},{"id":"d","text":"The book is more liquid on the bid side, so selling is cheaper than buying.","correct":false,"explanation":"Depth totals over ten levels do not tell you the spread or how the quantity is distributed by distance from mid. Liquidity needs that detail.","misconceptionId":"bigger-book-always-more-liquid"}]},{"id":"kc.orderbook.price-time-priority.1","skillId":"orderbook.price-time-priority","prompt":"At the best bid, order X (0.2 BTC, placed 10:00:01) and order Y (1.0 BTC, placed 10:00:03) are resting. A market sell of 0.5 BTC arrives. How is it filled?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.5 against Y, because Y is larger.","correct":false,"explanation":"Size gives no priority. At the same price, the earlier order fills first.","misconceptionId":"earlier-order-loses-to-bigger"},{"id":"b","text":"0.2 against X, then 0.3 against Y.","correct":true,"explanation":"Same price, so time decides: X arrived first and is filled in full, then the remainder goes to Y."},{"id":"c","text":"Split in proportion to size: about 0.08 to X and 0.42 to Y.","correct":false,"explanation":"Pro-rata matching exists on some venues, but this book uses price-time priority, which fills the earliest order first.","misconceptionId":"earlier-order-loses-to-bigger"},{"id":"d","text":"The sell is rejected because 0.5 is not a multiple of the tick size.","correct":false,"explanation":"Tick size applies to price, not quantity. Quantity must be a multiple of the lot size, and 0.5 is a valid multiple of any lot size that divides it.","misconceptionId":"tick-size-is-min-order"}]},{"id":"kc.orderbook.spot-vs-perps.1","skillId":"orderbook.spot-vs-perps","prompt":"For the same asset, the perp book shows a 0.02% spread with about 2 units per level; the spot book shows a 0.20% spread with about 30 units per level. You want to buy 60 units at once. Which statement is best?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The perp book is cheaper because its spread is ten times tighter.","correct":false,"explanation":"The spread prices only the first small slice. Sixty units would walk about thirty perp levels, while spot fills them within two.","misconceptionId":"tighter-spread-best-for-any-size"},{"id":"b","text":"Compare the average fill price for 60 units on each book; spot''s deeper levels may make it cheaper despite the wider spread.","correct":true,"explanation":"For a given size, spread and depth together set the cost. That needs the average fill on each book, not the spread alone."},{"id":"c","text":"The two books must show the same price, so either is fine.","correct":false,"explanation":"They are separate markets with separate orders. Funding keeps the perp near spot, but their quotes and depth differ.","misconceptionId":"perps-price-is-spot-price"},{"id":"d","text":"The spot book is always better because it trades the real token.","correct":false,"explanation":"For a small order the perp''s tighter spread can be cheaper. Which book is better depends on the size, and the perp also carries funding, which the book does not show."}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.orderbook.cost-of-a-trade","skillIds":["orderbook.bid-ask","orderbook.spread","orderbook.depth"],"kind":"orderbook","title":"What would this trade cost?","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"Read a real book, compute spread and estimate the fill of a given market order, with the right caveats.","evidenceRequirements":["orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC"],"questions":["State the best bid, best ask, mid price and spread, in ticks and as a percentage of mid.","Walk the ask side for a market buy of 0.5 BTC. Give the worst price, the average fill price and the impact over the best ask. Show the levels you used.","Do the same for a market sell of 0.5 BTC on the bid side. Is the book symmetric for this size?","What two things could make your estimate wrong by the time an order arrived?"],"expectedReasoning":["Reads best levels and computes spread correctly","Walks cumulative depth level by level","Reports worst and average prices separately","States that the book is a snapshot and can change"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Reads best bid, best ask and mid correctly","Computes the spread in ticks using the market''s tick size and as a percentage of mid","Walks the ask side correctly and reports the right worst price for 0.5 BTC","Computes a quantity-weighted average fill price","Walks the bid side correctly for the sell case"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the levels used, with price and quantity, from the pack''s book","Cites the book''s timestamp (block time) and provenance","Uses the market''s stated tick size rather than a guess","Uses cumulative quantity to show where 0.5 BTC is reached"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Distinguishes worst price from average price and explains why both matter","Compares the two sides and says whether the book is symmetric for this size","Explains impact as a percentage over the best price","Notes that the exchange fee would be added on top"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["States that resting orders can be cancelled or added before an order arrives","Notes that the book shows a limited number of levels","Says that the estimate holds only for the snapshot''s block time","Avoids presenting the estimate as a guaranteed execution"]}],"keyPoints":["the spread is best ask minus best bid","spread divided by mid gives a percentage comparable across markets","a market buy walks the ask side from the best ask outwards","the worst price is the last level touched","the average fill price is quantity weighted across the levels used","the exchange fee is separate from the spread and the impact","the book is a snapshot at one block time and can change"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Reporting impact over mid instead of over best ask, if stated","Using a different order size if the book is too thin for 0.5 BTC, with the reason given","Expressing the spread in quote currency as well as ticks"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["mid-price-is-tradeable","spread-is-fee","depth-is-static"]},"hints":["Start at the best ask and add quantities until you reach 0.5. The price where you stop is the worst price.","For the average, multiply each level''s price by the quantity you took there, add them up and divide by 0.5.","A full answer lists best bid, ask, mid, spread (ticks and %), the levels walked on each side with worst and average prices, says whether the sides match, and ends with ''if the book is unchanged at the moment the order lands''."]},{"id":"ex.orderbook.two-books","skillIds":["orderbook.liquidity","orderbook.imbalance","orderbook.depth"],"kind":"orderbook","title":"Which book is more liquid?","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"Compare two books on spread and depth within a band, compute imbalance, and refuse to read imbalance as a forecast.","evidenceRequirements":["orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC","orderbook:vETH_vUSDC"],"questions":["For each book, give the spread as a percentage of mid and the quantity within 0.5% of mid on each side, in quote currency so the two markets are comparable.","Which book is more liquid for an order worth 10,000 in quote currency? Why is total book size not the right basis for this answer?","Compute bid share of depth over the top ten levels for each book. Write one sentence saying what each number does and does not tell you.","If a news item claimed one of these books showed ''strong support'' or ''heavy selling'', how would you respond using the data?"],"expectedReasoning":["Normalises depth to quote currency within a band","Judges liquidity by spread and near-mid depth, not total size","Computes bid share of depth correctly","Treats imbalance as a description, not a forecast"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Computes spread as a percentage of mid for both books","Sums depth within 0.5% of mid correctly for both sides of both books","Converts base quantity to quote value using level prices","Computes bid share of depth over ten levels correctly","Picks the more liquid book for the stated size with a correct justification"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the levels and timestamps used from both books","Uses each market''s own tick size and mid","Notes whether the two snapshots were taken at the same time","States how many levels each book contains"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why quote-currency depth in a band is the right comparison","Explains why far-from-mid quantity does not help an ordinary order","Explains what bid share of depth describes and why it cannot forecast","Rebuts ''support'' or ''selling pressure'' language with the snapshot argument"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Notes that both books can change before any order arrives","Notes that the comparison holds for the stated order size only","Says that resting orders may be placed and pulled without trading","Avoids any directional claim"]}],"keyPoints":["liquidity is spread and depth near the mid together","depth should be compared within a fixed band such as half a percent of mid","convert base quantity to quote value to compare markets","total book size is dominated by far-away orders","bid share of depth is bids divided by bids plus asks over stated levels","imbalance describes resting orders at one block time","resting orders can be cancelled and do not forecast the next trade"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using a 0.25% or 1% band with the choice stated","Using five levels for imbalance if the book has fewer than ten on a side","Concluding that neither book is liquid enough for the size, with figures"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["bigger-book-always-more-liquid","imbalance-predicts-price","liquidity-equals-volume","depth-is-static"]},"hints":["Fix the band first (0.5% each side of mid), then add up only the levels inside it. Multiply quantity by price to get quote value.","Bid share = bid quantity ÷ (bid + ask quantity) over the same number of levels on each side.","A full answer gives spread % and in-band depth for both books, names the more liquid one for 10,000 of quote with the band figures, gives both bid shares with a ''snapshot, not forecast'' sentence, and answers the news claim by pointing out that resting orders can be pulled."]},{"id":"ex.orderbook.valid-order-and-queue","skillIds":["orderbook.tick-lot","orderbook.price-time-priority","orderbook.bid-ask"],"kind":"orderbook","title":"Is this order valid, and where does it queue?","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"Check proposed orders against tick size, lot size, minimum quantity and minimum notional, and work out their place under price-time priority.","evidenceRequirements":["orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC"],"questions":["Using the market''s tick size and lot size from the pack, check these proposed orders and say which are valid: (a) a bid at the best bid plus half a tick; (b) a bid of 0.000015 BTC at the best bid; (c) a bid of 0.00005 BTC at the best bid if the minimum notional is 5 in quote currency.","Suppose you place a valid limit bid at the current best bid. Where does it sit in the queue at that level, and what has to happen before it fills?","Suppose instead you place a bid one tick above the best bid. What changes about your priority, and what does it cost you?","Explain why a large order at the same price as an earlier small order does not jump ahead of it."],"expectedReasoning":["Applies tick and lot rules separately to price and quantity","Applies minimum notional as a separate check","Reasons about queue position under price-time priority","Trades off priority against price"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Rejects a price that is not a tick multiple","Rejects a quantity that is not a lot multiple","Rejects a lot-valid quantity whose notional is below the minimum, computing the notional from the book''s price","States that an order at the best bid queues behind existing quantity at that level","States that an order one tick above becomes the new best bid with first priority at that price"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the market''s tick size and lot size from the pack","Uses the book''s actual best bid and resting quantity at that level","Computes notional using a price from the book","Notes the book''s timestamp"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains the difference between the price grid and the quantity grid","Explains why minimum notional is a separate check from lot size","Explains what must trade before a queued order fills","Explains the cost of improving the price by one tick in terms of the spread"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Notes that queue position depends on orders that may be cancelled ahead","Notes that the minimum notional and minimum quantity values must be read from the venue''s symbol data","Says that a resting order may never fill","Avoids claiming a specific time to fill"]}],"keyPoints":["prices must be integer multiples of the tick size","quantities must be integer multiples of the lot size","minimum notional is price times quantity and is a separate floor","an order at an existing level queues behind the quantity already there","an order one tick better becomes the best price and goes to the front at that price","at the same price the earlier order fills first regardless of size","a resting order fills only when an incoming order trades against it"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the pack''s actual minimum notional if present instead of the hypothetical 5","Describing a post-only order as the way to guarantee resting","Noting that stepping ahead by one tick narrows the spread for everyone"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["tick-size-is-min-order","earlier-order-loses-to-bigger","mid-price-is-tradeable"]},"hints":["Three separate checks: is the price on the grid, is the quantity on the grid, and is price × quantity big enough?","At a given price, imagine a queue. New orders join the back. What makes an order move to the front of a new queue instead?","A full answer rejects (a) for tick, (b) for lot, (c) for notional (0.00005 × price < 5), places the new bid behind the resting quantity at the best bid, explains that a one-tick-better bid is first in a new queue but pays one tick more, and states that priority is price then time, never size."]},{"id":"ex.orderbook.spot-vs-perps","skillIds":["orderbook.spot-vs-perps","orderbook.spread","orderbook.depth"],"kind":"orderbook","title":"Spot or perpetual: which book?","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"Compare the ANT/USDC spot book with the ANT-USD perpetual book on spread, quote-value depth and the cost of a stated size, and name what the books leave out.","evidenceRequirements":["orderbook:vANT_vUSDC","orderbook:perps:ANT-USD"],"questions":["Give the best bid, best ask and spread as a percentage of mid for each book. Which is tighter?","Add up the quote value (price × quantity) of the top ten levels on each side of each book. Which book has more value near the top?","For a buy worth about 2,000 in quote currency, walk each book''s asks and estimate the average price. Does the tighter book still win?","What does neither book show you about the cost of holding a perpetual position, and why is neither price ''the'' price of ANT?"],"expectedReasoning":["Computes spread as a percentage of mid for both books","Measures depth in quote value over the same levels","Walks the book for a stated size before choosing","Names funding as a cost the order book does not show"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Reads best bid and best ask correctly from both books","Computes spread as a percentage of mid correctly to two decimal places","Sums top-ten quote value correctly on both sides of both books","Estimates the average fill for the stated size from the level prices and quantities","States that spot and perp are separate markets with separate orders"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites both books by name and their timestamps","Quotes level prices and quantities used in the walk","Notes that the two books were captured at slightly different times","Notes the provenance of each book"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why spread alone prices only the first slice of an order","Explains why depth must be compared in quote value, not contracts or tokens","Links the answer to the stated order size","Explains that funding keeps the perp near spot but is not on the book"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says the books can change within seconds","Names funding and fees as costs not in the pack","Avoids calling either book the true price","Avoids any suggestion to trade either market"]}],"keyPoints":["spot and perpetual books are separate markets","spread as a percentage of mid compares the two books","compare depth in quote value","a tighter spread only prices the first slice of an order","walk the book for the stated size","funding is a cost the order book does not show","neither book alone is the price of the asset"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the top five levels instead of ten, if stated","Estimating the worst fill price instead of the average","Choosing a different order size and recomputing"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["perps-price-is-spot-price","tighter-spread-best-for-any-size","bigger-book-always-more-liquid","spread-is-fee"]},"hints":["Start with best bid and best ask on each book, then spread ÷ mid × 100.","Multiply each level''s price by its quantity before adding, so both books are measured in the same money.","A full answer gives both spreads in percent, both top-ten quote values, an average fill for the stated size on each book, and says funding is not on the book and neither price is ''the'' ANT price."]}],"rubricNotes":"These are written-reasoning exercises about a book; the interactive drills live in the Order Book Gym. Grade arithmetic strictly: spread, walks, averages and bid share should match the pack''s levels. Reasoning marks hinge on two refusals: not reading the spread as the venue''s fee, and not reading imbalance or ''walls'' as a forecast. Every estimate must carry the snapshot caveat to earn full uncertainty marks.","recommendedNext":"Finish with ''Reasoning like an analyst'', which pulls every data type together into a thesis with chosen evidence, labelled inferences, stated gaps, calibrated confidence and a written invalidation."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('reasoning', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 6, '{"id":"reasoning","sequence":6,"title":"Reasoning like an analyst","summary":"Turn evidence into a written analysis: a testable thesis, a short list of evidence that bears on it, observations kept apart from interpretations, a list of what is missing, a calibrated confidence, and a written condition that would prove you wrong.","learningObjectives":["Write a one-sentence thesis that is specific, bounded in time and testable.","Select the two or three pieces of evidence that bear on the thesis and give reasons for leaving the rest out.","Label every sentence of an analysis as an observation or an interpretation.","List what the evidence pack lacks and say which conclusions each gap weakens.","State a confidence level tied to the evidence, and write an invalidation condition with a number and a time window."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":["news","etf"],"skillIds":["reasoning.thesis","reasoning.evidence-selection","reasoning.observation-vs-interpretation","reasoning.missing-info","reasoning.uncertainty","reasoning.invalidation"],"concepts":[{"heading":"A thesis is a sentence that can be wrong","body":"A **thesis** is a single claim about the market that you are prepared to defend with evidence. A useful thesis has three properties:\n\n- **Specific**: it names an asset or a quantity and a direction or a relationship. \"BTC ETF demand is positive\" is specific. \"Things look good\" is not.\n- **Bounded in time**: it says over what window it applies. \"Over the last ten trading days\" or \"for the next week\".\n- **Testable**: there is some observation that would show it to be false.\n\nA thesis is not a prediction of price. It can be a claim about demand, about a relationship between two data series, about what a news item does or does not show, or about the quality of a market. The habit is the same: say one thing, say when, and say how you would know if you were wrong.\n\nWrite the thesis first, before the evidence, then check whether the evidence supports it. If you write the evidence first, you will tend to write a thesis that fits whatever you found, and it will sound convincing without being tested.","example":"Weak: ''ETF flows are bullish.'' Better: ''Over the last ten settled trading days, net BTC ETF inflows have been positive on at least seven days and the ten-day sum is above the prior ten-day sum; this suggests steady ETF demand, with no claim about price.'' The second sentence can be checked line by line and can be false."},{"heading":"Choose evidence; do not collect it","body":"An evidence pack contains more than any one thesis needs. The skill is to pick the two or three items that **bear on the claim**, and to say why the rest do not.\n\nEvidence bears on a claim when it would look different if the claim were false. If your thesis is about ETF demand, the flow series bears on it directly. The order book on one venue does not: it would look much the same whether ETF demand were positive or negative. Including it adds length, not support.\n\nFor each item you keep, write one line: what it shows, and why that matters to the thesis. For each item you leave out, be ready to say in a few words why it does not bear on the claim. This keeps you honest in both directions: you cannot pad the argument, and you cannot quietly drop evidence that cuts against you.\n\n- Keep what would look different if the thesis were false.\n- Drop what would look the same either way.\n- Name at least one item that cuts against the thesis, if the pack has one.","example":"Suppose the thesis is about steady ETF demand. Keep: the flow series (direct), the ETF news item (it may describe a cause of flows). Drop: the order book (one venue, unrelated to creations and redemptions), the index snapshot (a basket return says nothing about ETF demand). Against: if ETH flows were negative over the same window, that cuts against a story of broad demand and must be mentioned."},{"heading":"Observation and interpretation in separate sentences","body":"An **observation** is something the data shows: \"24-hour volume was 12 billion, about 50% above the 30-day average.\" An **interpretation** is what you make of it: \"I read this as a repricing with wide participation.\"\n\nAnalysts get into trouble when the two are written in the same voice. \"Buyers stepped in\" sounds like an observation but is an interpretation; no dataset shows buyers stepping in, because every trade has a buyer and a seller. \"The market is nervous\", \"whales are accumulating\", \"sellers are exhausted\" are all interpretations wearing the clothes of facts.\n\nThe fix is mechanical. Write observations with the number, the source and the time. Write interpretations in a sentence that begins with a marker: \"I infer\", \"this is consistent with\", \"one reading is\". A reader can then check every observation and argue with every interpretation. That is what makes an analysis useful to someone other than its author.\n\n- Observation: number, source, time.\n- Interpretation: a marked sentence, with the observation it rests on.\n- Never let an interpretation stand alone as if it were seen.","example":"Mixed: ''Strong inflows show institutions are back.'' Separated: ''Observation: ten-day net BTC ETF inflow was +830 million, with eight positive days (provider, settled through yesterday). Interpretation: this is consistent with steady ETF demand; the data does not say who the buyers are, so \"institutions\" is a guess I cannot support from the pack.''"},{"heading":"Say what is missing","body":"Every evidence pack is partial. A snapshot covers spot but not derivatives. A news feed covers what was published, not what happened. An order book covers one venue at one block. Flow data is a day late. An index snapshot gives weights but not member returns.\n\nBefore you conclude, write a short list of what you would want and do not have, and next to each item say which conclusion it weakens. This is not a disclaimer to tack on the end. It changes what you can claim. If you cannot see intraday prices, you cannot say which came first, the headline or the move. If you cannot see member returns, you cannot say how broad an index move was.\n\nA good missing-information list is specific: not \"more data would help\" but \"intraday BTC prices around 14:00 UTC, to test whether the move preceded the article\". Specific gaps point at specific limits of the analysis, and they tell the reader exactly what evidence would move you.\n\n- List the gaps.\n- Link each gap to the conclusion it weakens.\n- Make each gap specific enough that someone could go and fill it.","example":"Suppose your thesis links a price fall to a regulatory item. Gaps: (1) intraday price path, which weakens any timing claim; (2) derivatives liquidation data, which weakens the claim that the article was the main driver; (3) a second source for the article, which weakens the factual basis. Each gap maps to a part of the argument that must be hedged."},{"heading":"Calibrated confidence and a written invalidation","body":"**Confidence** should follow evidence, not tone. Strong words (\"clearly\", \"definitely\") add nothing a reader can check. Instead, give a level (low, medium, high, or a rough percentage) and say what it rests on. Confidence rises with primary sources, with multiple independent lines of evidence, and with a mechanism you can describe. It falls with single sources, short windows, missing data and moves that are within normal range.\n\n**Invalidation** is the observation that would make you drop the thesis. Write it in advance, with a number and a time window: \"If net BTC ETF flows are negative on four of the next five trading days, I will drop the steady-demand thesis.\" A thesis with no invalidation cannot be wrong, which means it cannot be right either; any outcome can be read as confirming it.\n\nTogether, confidence and invalidation make an analysis honest and useful. The reader knows how much weight to put on it and exactly what would change your mind.\n\n- Confidence: a level, plus what it rests on, plus what would move it.\n- Invalidation: a specific observation, a number, a window.\n- No \"whatever happens, this confirms it\".","example":"''Confidence: medium. It rests on ten days of settled flow data from one provider and one news item; it would rise with a second provider''s series or a longer window, and fall if ETH flows diverge. Invalidation: negative net BTC ETF flow on four of the next five trading days, or a single day below −500 million.''"}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.reasoning.thesis.1","skillId":"reasoning.thesis","prompt":"Which of these is the best-formed thesis?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Crypto is looking strong.","correct":false,"explanation":"No asset, no quantity, no window, and nothing that could show it false. It cannot be tested."},{"id":"b","text":"Over the last ten settled trading days, net BTC ETF inflows were positive on at least seven days; this suggests steady ETF demand, with no claim about price.","correct":true,"explanation":"Specific (BTC ETF net inflow), bounded (ten settled days), testable (count the days), and it says what it does not claim."},{"id":"c","text":"BTC will rise because ETF inflows are strong.","correct":false,"explanation":"It is a price prediction built on a flows-to-price link the data cannot support, and it has no window.","misconceptionId":"flows-equal-price-impact"},{"id":"d","text":"Whatever happens this week, demand is clearly building.","correct":false,"explanation":"''Whatever happens'' means no outcome could show it false, and ''clearly'' is confidence without evidence.","misconceptionId":"thesis-without-invalidation"}]},{"id":"kc.reasoning.observation-vs-interpretation.1","skillId":"reasoning.observation-vs-interpretation","prompt":"Which sentence is an observation rather than an interpretation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Buyers stepped in at 58,000.","correct":false,"explanation":"No dataset shows ''buyers stepping in''. Every trade has a buyer and a seller. This is an interpretation of a price bounce.","misconceptionId":"observation-is-interpretation"},{"id":"b","text":"24-hour volume was 12 billion, about 50% above the 30-day average, per the provider snapshot at 09:00 UTC.","correct":true,"explanation":"Number, comparison, source and time. Anyone can check it, and it makes no claim about why."},{"id":"c","text":"The market is nervous ahead of the data release.","correct":false,"explanation":"''Nervous'' is a state of mind attributed to a market. It is an interpretation, and should be marked as one.","misconceptionId":"observation-is-interpretation"},{"id":"d","text":"Whales are accumulating, which is why the bid is deep.","correct":false,"explanation":"Who placed the bids is not in the book, and ''which is why'' asserts a cause. Two interpretations in one sentence.","misconceptionId":"imbalance-predicts-price"}]},{"id":"kc.reasoning.uncertainty.1","skillId":"reasoning.uncertainty","prompt":"An analysis rests on ten days of flow data from one provider and one news item citing an anonymous source. Which confidence statement is best?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"High confidence; the picture is clear and everyone agrees.","correct":false,"explanation":"''Clear'' and ''everyone agrees'' are tone, not evidence. One provider and one anonymous source do not support high confidence.","misconceptionId":"confidence-is-evidence"},{"id":"b","text":"Medium confidence; it rests on one provider''s ten-day series and one unconfirmed item, and would rise with a second series or a primary source.","correct":true,"explanation":"A level, what it rests on, and what would move it. This is calibrated to the evidence."},{"id":"c","text":"No confidence can be given because the future is uncertain.","correct":false,"explanation":"Uncertainty about the future does not stop you rating the evidence you have. Refusing to give a level is not calibration."},{"id":"d","text":"High confidence, because two outlets published the news item.","correct":false,"explanation":"If both outlets rely on the same anonymous source, that is one source. Repetition does not raise confidence.","misconceptionId":"single-source-certainty"}]},{"id":"kc.reasoning.invalidation.1","skillId":"reasoning.invalidation","prompt":"The thesis is ''ETF demand for BTC is steady''. Which is a proper invalidation condition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"If price falls, that confirms sellers are exhausted; if it rises, demand is proven.","correct":false,"explanation":"Both outcomes are read as confirmation. A thesis that nothing could falsify is not an analysis.","misconceptionId":"thesis-without-invalidation"},{"id":"b","text":"Negative net BTC ETF flow on four of the next five trading days, or any single day below −500 million.","correct":true,"explanation":"A specific observation, with a number and a window, in the same data series the thesis is about."},{"id":"c","text":"If I change my mind.","correct":false,"explanation":"An invalidation must be an observation in the world, not a feeling. Otherwise it cannot be set in advance.","misconceptionId":"thesis-without-invalidation"},{"id":"d","text":"If BTC falls 10% this week.","correct":false,"explanation":"A price fall is not evidence about ETF demand; flows could stay positive through a fall. The condition must test the thesis, not something else.","misconceptionId":"flows-equal-price-impact"}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.reasoning.thesis-and-evidence","skillIds":["reasoning.thesis","reasoning.evidence-selection","reasoning.observation-vs-interpretation"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Write a thesis and choose your evidence","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"State a testable thesis about ETF demand, select the evidence that bears on it, and keep observations and interpretations apart.","evidenceRequirements":["etf:BTC","etf:ETH","news:etf","snapshot:BTC","index:all"],"questions":["Write a one-sentence thesis about ETF demand for BTC over the last ten settled trading days. It must name a quantity, a window and something that could show it false.","From the pack, pick the two or three items that bear on the thesis. For each, say what it shows and why it matters. Name at least one item you are leaving out and why.","Write your observations as separate sentences with number, source and time. Then write your interpretations as marked sentences.","Does anything in the pack cut against your thesis? Say so."],"expectedReasoning":["Produces a specific, bounded, testable thesis","Selects evidence by whether it would look different if the thesis were false","Separates observation and interpretation in form, not only in word","Reports evidence that cuts against the thesis"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["The thesis names a quantity, a window and a falsifying observation","Observations quote figures from the pack correctly","The ETF fields are described correctly (net inflow, cumulative, net assets)","The thesis makes no claim about price impact","Items left out are described correctly as not bearing on ETF demand"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Keeps the BTC flow series as the primary evidence","Uses the ETH series or the ETF news item as a second line, with a reason","Explicitly drops at least one item (for example the index snapshot) with a reason","Cites dates, provider and settlement status"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains the ''would it look different if false'' test for each kept item","Writes interpretations as marked sentences resting on named observations","Reports contrary evidence rather than omitting it","Keeps ''who is buying'' out of the claims unless the pack supports it"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Notes the T+1 lag and the single-provider source","Avoids words such as ''clearly'' and ''definitely''","States that the thesis is about demand for the ETF wrapper only","Says what would strengthen the evidence base"]}],"keyPoints":["a thesis must be specific bounded in time and testable","evidence bears on a claim if it would look different were the claim false","the btc flow series is the direct evidence for an etf demand thesis","the order book and index snapshot do not bear on etf demand","observations carry a number a source and a time","interpretations are marked as the analyst''s reading","contrary evidence must be reported not dropped"],"acceptableAlternatives":["A thesis about ETH ETF demand instead of BTC, if the pack supports it","A thesis that demand is weak or mixed, if the flow series supports it","Keeping the BTC snapshot as context for net assets, with a reason"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["more-evidence-is-stronger","observation-is-interpretation","flows-equal-price-impact","narrative-is-evidence"]},"hints":["Write the thesis before you look at the evidence in detail. Then ask of each item: would this look different if my thesis were false?","For every sentence you write, ask: is this a number from the pack, or is this what I think the number means? Put them in separate sentences.","A full answer has a thesis with a count and a window, two or three kept items each with a reason, one dropped item with a reason, observation sentences with sources and times, marked interpretation sentences, and a line about any contrary data such as ETH flows."]},{"id":"ex.reasoning.gaps-and-confidence","skillIds":["reasoning.missing-info","reasoning.uncertainty","reasoning.evidence-selection"],"kind":"scenario","title":"What is missing, and how sure are you?","difficulty":5,"learningObjective":"Analyse a possible news-to-price link, list the specific gaps in the evidence, map each gap to the conclusion it weakens, and state a calibrated confidence.","evidenceRequirements":["snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH","news:market","news:macro","orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC"],"questions":["State the strongest claim you could make linking a news item in the pack to BTC''s 24-hour change. Write it in ''consistent with'' form.","List at least three specific pieces of information the pack does not contain, and for each say which part of your claim it weakens.","Give a confidence level for the claim. Say exactly what it rests on and what would raise or lower it.","Which item in the pack is least relevant to this question, and why might a weaker analysis have included it anyway?"],"expectedReasoning":["Frames the claim as consistent-with, not causal","Produces specific, fillable gaps","Maps each gap to a conclusion","Calibrates confidence to sources, windows and mechanism"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Reads the 24-hour change and the item''s publish time correctly","States correctly that a snapshot cannot show intraday timing","States correctly that the snapshot covers spot and not derivatives","Identifies the order book as one venue at one block","Does not assert causation"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the news item, its publisher and its source type","Cites the snapshot timestamp and the book''s block time","Uses the ETH snapshot to check whether the move was market-wide","Uses the macro feed to look for a competing driver"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Each gap is specific enough that someone could go and fill it","Each gap is tied to the conclusion it weakens","Confidence is justified by source quality, window length and mechanism, not by tone","Explains why the least relevant item is tempting to include"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Gives a level and what would move it","Keeps alternatives (macro, market-wide, noise) on the page","Avoids ''clearly'', ''definitely'' and similar words","Notes that the move may be within normal range and need no explanation"]}],"keyPoints":["consistent with is the strongest honest form for a news-to-price link","intraday prices are needed to test timing","derivatives data is needed to test the main driver","a second independent source is needed to firm up the news fact","a gap weakens a specific conclusion not the analysis in general","confidence rests on source quality window length and mechanism","a move within normal range needs no special explanation"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Concluding that no item in the pack plausibly links to the move","Treating the ETH move as evidence for a market-wide driver","Using the order book as weak evidence of current liquidity rather than dropping it entirely"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["missing-data-is-neutral","confidence-is-evidence","correlation-is-causation","more-evidence-is-stronger"]},"hints":["Write the claim first. Then, for each part of it (timing, size, mechanism, the fact itself), ask what data you would need to check that part.","A gap is only useful if it points at a conclusion. ''We lack intraday prices'' matters because it stops you saying which came first.","A full answer has a ''consistent with'' claim, three specific gaps each tied to a conclusion, a confidence level with its basis and movers, and a note that the order book (one venue, one block) is the least relevant item here."]},{"id":"ex.reasoning.full-brief","skillIds":["reasoning.thesis","reasoning.invalidation","reasoning.uncertainty","reasoning.observation-vs-interpretation","reasoning.missing-info"],"kind":"scenario","title":"The full brief","difficulty":5,"learningObjective":"Produce a complete short analysis from the whole pack: thesis, evidence, observations and interpretations, gaps, confidence, and a written invalidation.","evidenceRequirements":["snapshot:BTC","snapshot:ETH","news:market","news:etf","etf:BTC","index:all","orderbook:vBTC_vUSDC"],"questions":["Write a thesis about the current state of the BTC market (demand, liquidity or the news-to-price relationship) that is specific, bounded in time and testable.","Present the evidence in two labelled blocks: observations (number, source, time) and interpretations (marked).","List the gaps in the pack and the conclusions each gap weakens.","State your confidence and what it rests on.","Write an invalidation condition with a number and a time window in the same data series as your thesis.","What would improve this analysis most, if you could add one dataset?"],"expectedReasoning":["Produces a thesis that is specific, bounded and testable","Keeps observation and interpretation in separate labelled blocks","Lists gaps mapped to conclusions","Writes an invalidation that tests the thesis in its own data series"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["The thesis is specific, bounded and testable","Observations quote the pack''s figures correctly across at least three data types","The ETF, index and order book fields used are described correctly","No figure is presented as a value judgement or a price forecast","The invalidation is in the same data series as the thesis"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Uses only the items that bear on the thesis and names those left out","Cites source, time and provenance for each observation","Reports any item that cuts against the thesis","Uses the ETH snapshot or index as a check on whether a move was market-wide, where relevant"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Interpretations are marked and each rests on a named observation","The argument moves from observations to thesis without a causal leap","Gaps are specific and tied to conclusions","The single most valuable missing dataset is chosen with a reason"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Confidence is a level with its basis and what would move it","The invalidation has a number and a window","Alternatives are kept on the page","No ''whatever happens'' or ''clearly'' language"]}],"keyPoints":["a thesis must be specific bounded in time and testable","observations are separated from interpretations in labelled blocks","evidence is chosen by whether it bears on the thesis","each gap weakens a named conclusion","confidence is a level tied to sources windows and mechanism","an invalidation names a number and a time window in the thesis''s own data","contrary evidence is reported rather than dropped"],"acceptableAlternatives":["A thesis about liquidity on the venue, with invalidation in spread or depth terms","A thesis that the news-to-price link cannot be established, with invalidation as the arrival of specific evidence","A thesis about ETF demand, with invalidation in flow terms"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["thesis-without-invalidation","confidence-is-evidence","observation-is-interpretation","correlation-is-causation","flows-equal-price-impact","imbalance-predicts-price"]},"hints":["Pick one thing to claim. Demand, liquidity, or the news link. Not all three.","Your invalidation must be measurable in the same data your thesis is about. A flows thesis needs a flows invalidation, not a price one.","A full brief has: one thesis sentence; an observations block with sources and times; a marked interpretations block; a gap list with the conclusion each weakens; ''confidence: level, basis, movers''; ''invalidation: number, window, series''; and one named dataset that would help most."]}],"rubricNotes":"This module grades form as much as content. A learner can get the facts right and still fail the reasoning dimension by mixing observations with interpretations, padding with irrelevant evidence, or writing a thesis nothing could falsify. Cap the uncertainty score for any answer with no invalidation condition or with confidence expressed through tone. Reward answers that report contrary evidence unprompted.","recommendedNext":"You have finished the foundations track. Return to any module where your mastery is below target, or use the Order Book Gym and live scenarios to keep the habits sharp with fresh data."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;
insert into public.modules (id, learning_path_id, sequence, content) values ('wealth', 'crypto-market-analyst-foundations', 7, '{"id":"wealth","sequence":7,"title":"Funds, yield and incentives","summary":"How to read a board of funds: tokenised ETFs, vaults and an index fund; NAV against traded price; fees and fee drag; what an APY or a past return can and cannot tell you; dollar-cost averaging; diversification across funds that move together; and how trading rewards inflate volume and colour what you see.","learningObjectives":["Tell apart a tokenised ETF on an order book, an escrow vault, a yield vault and an index fund, and say what drives each one''s price and risk.","Compare NAV with traded price, including when each was last updated and whether the underlying market is open.","Work out fee drag and read APY, ROI and simulated DCA returns as descriptions of the past, not promises.","Measure concentration across a set of funds and judge diversification by co-movement, not by count.","Explain how points multipliers and volume-based reward boxes inflate trading volume, and why a public win feed is a biased sample."],"prerequisiteModuleIds":["etf","indexes"],"skillIds":["wealth.fund-types","wealth.nav-vs-price","wealth.fees","wealth.yield-apy","wealth.dca","wealth.diversification","wealth.incentives"],"concepts":[{"heading":"Four kinds of fund on one board","body":"A wealth board can list very different products side by side, and the same columns mean different things for each. Before you read any number, find out what kind of fund you are looking at.\n\n- A **tokenised ETF on the order book** is a token that tracks a traditional exchange-traded fund, such as one following a US share index. It trades on a central limit order book against a stablecoin, so its price comes from bids and asks. Its underlying shares only trade during market hours.\n- An **escrow vault** holds deposits for a stated strategy and reports a net asset value per share. Subscriptions and redemptions follow the vault''s own windows and rules.\n- A **yield vault**, such as a bridged USDC vault, lends a stablecoin out and passes on the interest. Its share value creeps up slowly; its risks are about the lending venue, not price swings.\n- An **index fund**, such as the ANT Believers Index, holds a weighted basket of crypto assets and publishes a NAV that moves with them, around the clock.\n\nEach type carries its own risks. Grouping them under one word, \"funds\", hides that.","example":"Suppose a board shows four rows: a tokenised share-index ETF up 1.5% today, a USDC yield vault whose share value rose 0.01%, an escrow vault up 11%, and a crypto index up 2.4%. The ETF move reflects share prices, the vault move reflects a day of interest, the escrow move reflects its own strategy, and the index move reflects its crypto members. Four numbers, four different stories."},{"heading":"NAV and traded price are two different numbers","body":"**Net asset value (NAV)** per share is what the fund''s holdings are worth, divided by the number of shares, calculated at a stated time. The **traded price** is what a buyer and seller last agreed on a market. They are linked but not the same.\n\nWhen the price is above NAV the fund trades at a **premium**; below NAV it trades at a **discount**. Premium or discount = (price − NAV) ÷ NAV. Small gaps are normal. Large or persistent gaps say something about demand, redemption rules or stale data.\n\nAlways check the **timestamps**. A NAV struck an hour ago and a price from a minute ago can differ simply because the underlying assets moved in between. A tokenised ETF has a further twist: when its home stock market is closed, the token can still trade, but the shares it tracks are not moving, so the quoted price may be someone''s guess about the next open rather than a measured value.\n\nReading routine:\n\n- Note NAV, price and both times.\n- Compute the premium or discount.\n- Ask whether the underlying market was open at both times.","example":"Suppose an index fund shows NAV 9.60 at 09:00 and a traded price of 9.70 at 09:45. The premium is (9.70 − 9.60) ÷ 9.60 ≈ 1.0%. Before calling that a premium, check whether the index members rose between 09:00 and 09:45; if they rose 1%, the gap is just timing."},{"heading":"Fees and fee drag","body":"Funds charge fees, and fees compound against you. A **management fee** is a yearly percentage of what you hold, taken whether the fund rises or falls. A **performance fee** is a share of gains above some mark, taken only when the fund does well.\n\nThe effect of a fee over time is called **fee drag**. A 1% yearly fee does not cost 1% once; it costs 1% every year on a growing (or shrinking) balance. Over long periods the gap between a low-fee and a high-fee version of the same holdings becomes large.\n\nTwo traps to avoid:\n\n- **Small numbers feel harmless.** A difference of 0.5% a year looks tiny next to a 10% daily move, but daily moves come and go while fees are certain and repeat.\n- **Unknown is not zero.** If a board or fund page leaves the fee blank, you do not know the fee. Treat it as missing information, not as free.\n\nWhen you compare funds, put fees next to returns and say over which period the comparison holds.","example":"Suppose two funds both earn 6% a year before fees for ten years on a hypothetical 1,000. Fund A charges 0.2% a year, fund B 2%. A ends near 1,000 × 1.058^10 ≈ 1,757; B near 1,000 × 1.04^10 ≈ 1,480. The same holdings, a gap of about 277, all from fees."},{"heading":"APY, ROI and what a 7-day APY cannot tell you","body":"**ROI** (return on investment) is how much something actually gained or lost over a stated past period: 1 month, 1 year, since launch. **APY** (annual percentage yield) takes a return over some window and scales it up to a year, with compounding.\n\nThat scaling is where people go wrong. A **7-day APY** takes one week and pretends it repeats 52 times. For a stablecoin yield vault earning steady interest, that can be a fair summary. For anything whose price swings, it is meaningless: a share-index ETF that rose 5% in a week would show an APY in the hundreds of percent, and a board might show 7-day APYs in the thousands for ordinary ETFs. Nobody earns that.\n\nFor a yield vault, ask **where the yield comes from**. Usually it is interest paid by borrowers on a lending venue. A label such as \"net excluding rewards\" means fees are taken out and token rewards are not counted. The risks are a smart-contract bug, borrowers failing or a venue running short of cash to withdraw, and rates simply falling. **An APY is a description of the recent past, never a promise.**","example":"Suppose a USDC vault shows 7-day APY 4.4% and 1-month APY 5.2%, while an ETF row shows a 7-day APY of 1,500%. The vault figures describe interest that varies a little week to week. The ETF figure is one week''s price move scaled up to a year; it says nothing about what a year will bring."},{"heading":"Dollar-cost averaging and simulated returns","body":"**Dollar-cost averaging (DCA)** means investing the same amount at regular intervals, say every week, instead of all at once (a **lump sum**). Because each contribution buys more units when the price is low and fewer when it is high, the average cost per unit ends up below the simple average price over the period.\n\nNeither approach always wins. If the price rises steadily, a lump sum put in at the start does better, because more money was exposed to the rise for longer. If the price falls and then recovers, DCA often does better. Which one wins depends entirely on the path the price took.\n\nA board may show a **DCA simulation**: the return a hypothetical weekly equal-contribution plan would have had over a range such as one year. Read it carefully:\n\n- Check the **window it actually covers**. The last data point may be months before today, even when the label says \"1Y\".\n- It is **one path**. A different start date gives a different number.\n- **Past simulated returns do not predict** future returns.","example":"Suppose a price goes 100, 80, 100 over three weeks and you invest 100 each week. You buy 1, 1.25 and 1 units: 3.25 units for 300, worth 325 at the end, +8.3%. A 300 lump sum at the start buys 3 units worth 300, 0%. If the path were 100, 120, 100, DCA would lose and the lump sum would break even."},{"heading":"Diversification across funds","body":"Owning several funds is not the same as being diversified. What matters, as with index members, is **how the holdings move together** and **how the weight is spread**.\n\nThree share-index ETFs that are all heavy in large US technology companies will rise and fall almost together. Holding all three spreads you across three tickers but not across three risks. A fund that holds mostly short-term bills, a stablecoin yield vault and a crypto index behave very differently from each other; mixing them spreads risk far more, though each brings its own.\n\nMeasure concentration with numbers:\n\n- **Largest weight.** If one fund is more than 50% of the portfolio, the portfolio mostly behaves like that fund.\n- **HHI** (Herfindahl-Hirschman index): square each weight in percent and add them up. 10,000 means everything is in one fund.\n- **Effective number of holdings** = 10,000 ÷ HHI. Four equal weights give 4; weights of 70, 10, 10, 10 give about 1.9.\n\nAlso look at **themes and hours**: if every fund tracks the same theme, or every fund depends on one stock market being open, a single event hits all of them at once.","example":"Suppose a practice portfolio is 40% in a share-index ETF, 30% in a technology ETF, 20% in a semiconductor ETF and 10% in a USDC vault. HHI = 1,600 + 900 + 400 + 100 = 3,000, so effective holdings ≈ 3.3. Yet 90% of the weight sits in three funds that largely move together, all trading on US market hours."},{"heading":"Trading incentives and what they do to the numbers","body":"Exchanges often pay traders to trade. Two common forms:\n\n- **Points multipliers.** Trading volume on chosen markets earns points at, say, 1.2 times the normal rate. Traders move activity to those markets to earn more.\n- **Volume-based reward boxes.** Every so many dollars of trading volume earns a box with a random reward. Volume from taking liquidity (a **taker**) may count in full, while volume from resting orders (a **maker**) counts at a lower weight, say 20%.\n\nBoth raise volume for reasons other than wanting to own the asset. Some traders buy and sell back and forth just to reach the next box. So on a boosted market, **high volume is not evidence of demand**. It may be mostly reward-seeking, and it can vanish when the campaign ends.\n\nWatch the public **win feed** too. If it only lists wins above a threshold, say 50 dollars, it shows you the lucky tail, not the typical box: a textbook case of **survivorship bias**. If the drop probabilities are **not published**, you cannot work out the expected value of a box at all. Say so rather than guessing.","example":"Suppose a campaign gives one box per 10,000 dollars of counted volume, with maker volume counted at 20%. A maker would need 50,000 dollars of resting-order fills for one box. If the feed shows ten wins of 100 to 1,000 dollars today, that tells you nothing about how many boxes paid 1 dollar."}],"knowledgeChecks":[{"id":"kc.wealth.fund-types.1","skillId":"wealth.fund-types","prompt":"A board lists a tokenised share-index ETF, a USDC yield vault and a crypto index fund. Which statement is most accurate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"They are all funds, so they carry the same kind of risk.","correct":false,"explanation":"The ETF moves with share prices and market hours, the vault depends on a lending venue, and the index moves with crypto prices around the clock. Different products, different risks.","misconceptionId":"fund-types-same-risk"},{"id":"b","text":"Each is driven by something different: share prices, lending interest, and a basket of crypto assets.","correct":true,"explanation":"Naming what drives each fund is the first step before comparing any of their numbers."},{"id":"c","text":"The yield vault is risk-free because its share value barely moves.","correct":false,"explanation":"A steady share value hides different risks: a contract bug, borrowers failing, or the venue running short of cash for withdrawals.","misconceptionId":"apy-is-guaranteed"},{"id":"d","text":"The index fund is diversified because it is called an index.","correct":false,"explanation":"Being an index says nothing about how its members move. Crypto members tend to move together, and weights may be concentrated.","misconceptionId":"concentration-is-diversification"}]},{"id":"kc.wealth.nav-vs-price.1","skillId":"wealth.nav-vs-price","prompt":"An index fund shows NAV 9.60 struck at 09:00 and a traded price of 9.70 at 09:45. What is the best reading?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"NAV and price are the same thing, so one of the figures is wrong.","correct":false,"explanation":"NAV is the value of the holdings per share at one time; price is the last agreed trade. They can differ without either being wrong.","misconceptionId":"nav-equals-price"},{"id":"b","text":"The fund trades at a premium of about 1.0%, but part or all of it may be timing, since the two figures are 45 minutes apart.","correct":true,"explanation":"(9.70 − 9.60) ÷ 9.60 ≈ 1.0%. Checking whether the members moved between 09:00 and 09:45 comes before calling it a premium."},{"id":"c","text":"The price above NAV shows buyers are confident the fund will rise.","correct":false,"explanation":"A premium is a gap between two numbers, not a forecast. It may come from timing, redemption rules or thin trading.","misconceptionId":"observation-is-interpretation"},{"id":"d","text":"The fund is undervalued by 10 cents.","correct":false,"explanation":"Price is above NAV here, so if anything it trades at a premium. And neither number is a statement of what the fund is ''really'' worth.","misconceptionId":"nav-equals-price"}]},{"id":"kc.wealth.fees.1","skillId":"wealth.fees","prompt":"Two funds with the same holdings earn 6% a year before fees for ten years on 1,000. Fund A charges 0.2% a year and fund B 2%. Which is closest to the truth?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The difference is about 18, because 1.8% of 1,000 is 18.","correct":false,"explanation":"Fees are taken every year on a growing balance. A ends near 1,757 and B near 1,480, a gap of about 277.","misconceptionId":"fees-dont-matter"},{"id":"b","text":"A ends near 1,757 and B near 1,480, so fees cost B about 277 over ten years.","correct":true,"explanation":"1,000 × 1.058^10 ≈ 1,757 and 1,000 × 1.04^10 ≈ 1,480. That compounding gap is fee drag."},{"id":"c","text":"There is no real difference, because a 2% fee is tiny next to daily price moves.","correct":false,"explanation":"Daily moves come and go; fees are certain and repeat every year. Over ten years they add up to a large gap.","misconceptionId":"fees-dont-matter"},{"id":"d","text":"If a third fund leaves the fee blank, it is the cheapest of the three.","correct":false,"explanation":"A blank fee is unknown, not zero. Treat it as missing information.","misconceptionId":"missing-data-is-neutral"}]},{"id":"kc.wealth.yield-apy.1","skillId":"wealth.yield-apy","prompt":"A board shows a USDC vault with 7-day APY 4.4% and a share-index ETF with 7-day APY 1,500%. What is the best reading?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The ETF will return about 1,500% over the next year.","correct":false,"explanation":"That figure takes one week''s price move and scales it to a year. It is not a forecast and nobody earns it.","misconceptionId":"short-window-apy-is-annual"},{"id":"b","text":"The vault is guaranteed to pay 4.4% for the next year.","correct":false,"explanation":"An APY describes the recent past. Lending rates change, and the vault carries contract and liquidity risks.","misconceptionId":"apy-is-guaranteed"},{"id":"c","text":"The vault figure summarises recent interest; the ETF figure is one week''s price move scaled up and says little about a year.","correct":true,"explanation":"A short-window APY is a fair summary of steady interest but meaningless for something whose price swings."},{"id":"d","text":"The ETF is the better yield product because its APY is higher.","correct":false,"explanation":"The two figures measure different things. The ETF''s number is not yield at all; it is a scaled-up price move.","misconceptionId":"short-window-apy-is-annual"}]},{"id":"kc.wealth.dca.1","skillId":"wealth.dca","prompt":"A weekly DCA simulation for a fund shows +7.6% over its window. Which statement is most accurate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A weekly plan started now will return about 7.6%.","correct":false,"explanation":"The simulation is one past path from one start date. Past simulated returns do not predict future ones.","misconceptionId":"past-return-predicts"},{"id":"b","text":"DCA beats a lump sum, so the lump sum would have done worse.","correct":false,"explanation":"Which approach wins depends on the price path. If the price rose steadily, the lump sum would have done better.","misconceptionId":"dca-always-wins"},{"id":"c","text":"It describes one hypothetical weekly plan over a stated window; check which dates it actually covers before comparing it with anything.","correct":true,"explanation":"The last data point may be months before today, and a different start date would give a different number."},{"id":"d","text":"The fund is safe because DCA smooths out risk.","correct":false,"explanation":"DCA spreads the timing of purchases. It does not remove the fund''s own risk; the plan can still end in a loss.","misconceptionId":"diversification-removes-risk"}]},{"id":"kc.wealth.diversification.1","skillId":"wealth.diversification","prompt":"A practice portfolio is 40% share-index ETF, 30% technology ETF, 20% semiconductor ETF and 10% USDC vault. Which description is best?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Four funds, so it is well diversified.","correct":false,"explanation":"Count is not diversification. Three of the four funds are heavy in the same companies and largely move together.","misconceptionId":"more-funds-is-diversified"},{"id":"b","text":"HHI 3,000, about 3.3 effective holdings, but 90% sits in three ETFs that largely move together on US market hours.","correct":true,"explanation":"1,600 + 900 + 400 + 100 = 3,000; 10,000 ÷ 3,000 ≈ 3.3. Naming the shared theme and shared hours completes the picture."},{"id":"c","text":"The USDC vault removes the portfolio''s risk.","correct":false,"explanation":"A 10% weight in a low-volatility vault dampens swings a little; it removes neither the ETFs'' risk nor the vault''s own.","misconceptionId":"diversification-removes-risk"},{"id":"d","text":"It is concentrated because the largest weight is over 50%.","correct":false,"explanation":"The largest weight is 40%, under 50%. The concentration problem here is the shared theme, not a single fund."}]},{"id":"kc.wealth.incentives.1","skillId":"wealth.incentives","prompt":"A market has a 1.2x points multiplier and counts towards reward boxes. Its volume doubled this week, and the public feed lists many wins of 100 dollars or more. What can you conclude?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Demand for the asset has doubled.","correct":false,"explanation":"Incentives pay people to trade. Some of the extra volume may be reward-seeking churn that disappears when the campaign ends.","misconceptionId":"incentive-volume-is-demand"},{"id":"b","text":"A typical box pays about 100 dollars.","correct":false,"explanation":"If the feed only lists wins above a threshold, it shows the lucky tail. Small rewards are left out.","misconceptionId":"feed-shows-typical-reward"},{"id":"c","text":"Volume rose, but incentives make it weak evidence of demand; the feed is filtered, and without published odds the value of a box is unknown.","correct":true,"explanation":"This separates what was observed from what it means and names the missing information."},{"id":"d","text":"Rising volume shows buyers are taking control.","correct":false,"explanation":"Every trade has a buyer and a seller. Volume measures activity, and here part of it is paid for.","misconceptionId":"volume-implies-direction"}]}],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.wealth.portfolio-review","skillIds":["wealth.diversification","wealth.fees","wealth.dca","wealth.fund-types"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Review your practice portfolio","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"Explain the choices in a paper portfolio using its computed analysis: concentration, fees, market hours and the DCA simulation, and say what would make you change it.","evidenceRequirements":["wealth:board","wealth:dca:vVOO:1Y","wealth:dca:vQQQ:1Y"],"questions":["Why did you choose these funds and these weights? Name each fund''s type and what drives it.","What concentration, fee and market-hours risks does the analysis show? Quote the numbers: largest weight, HHI or effective holdings, market-hours share and the weighted fee.","What does the DCA simulation tell you about these funds, and what does it not tell you?","What would make you change the portfolio? Name a specific observation, not a feeling."],"expectedReasoning":["Ties each fund to its type and what drives it","Quotes the computed concentration, hours and fee figures correctly","Treats the DCA simulation as one past path over a stated window","Treats missing fees or returns as unknown, not zero","States a concrete change trigger"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Describes each chosen fund''s type correctly: tokenised ETF, escrow vault, yield vault or index fund","Quotes the largest weight, HHI or effective holdings as given in the analysis","Quotes the market-hours share and weighted fee correctly, or says the fee is unknown when the analysis says so","Describes the DCA figures as simulated past returns, not expected returns","Does not call the portfolio diversified on the basis of fund count alone"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites fund ids and figures from the board as listed","Cites the DCA simulation returns and the dates they cover","Refers to the analysis flags by name","Notes the as-of time or provenance of the data"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why the weights were chosen in terms of risk, not expected gains","Links correlated or same-theme funds to weaker diversification","Explains why market-hours exposure matters for prices outside trading hours","Connects the change trigger to something observable in future data"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says past and simulated returns do not predict future returns","Treats unknown fees or missing returns as gaps, not as zero","Notes the DCA window may not match today or the stated range","Avoids certainty about how the portfolio will perform"]}],"keyPoints":["the effective number of holdings comes from the hhi","the largest weight shows how much one fund dominates","the market-hours share shows how much depends on a stock market being open","fees unknown is not the same as fees of zero","past simulated returns do not predict future returns","correlated funds are not diversification","a stated change trigger that can be observed"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the top-2 weight share instead of HHI, if the numbers are quoted correctly","Defending a concentrated portfolio as a deliberate learning choice, if the risk is named","Naming a rebalancing rule as the change trigger"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["more-funds-is-diversified","past-return-predicts","fees-dont-matter","missing-data-is-neutral","dca-always-wins"]},"hints":["Start with the analysis numbers in the scenario description: largest weight, effective holdings, market-hours share, fee.","For each fund, ask what drives it and whether it tends to move with your other funds. Then ask what the DCA simulation covers and what it leaves out.","A full answer names each fund''s type, quotes the concentration, hours and fee figures, says the DCA results are one past path that does not predict, treats unknown fees as unknown, and gives a concrete trigger such as ''if one fund passes 50% of the weight, I would rebalance the paper portfolio''."]},{"id":"ex.wealth.nav-vs-price","skillIds":["wealth.nav-vs-price","wealth.fund-types"],"kind":"scenario","title":"NAV or price?","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Compare an index fund''s NAV with its traded price, account for the times each was taken, and say what the gap can and cannot tell you.","evidenceRequirements":["wealth:board","wealth:fund:antb","wealth:price:antb:1M"],"questions":["For the ANT Believers Index, state the NAV, the quoted price and the time of each. Compute the premium or discount.","What kind of fund is it, and how does that affect whether NAV and price should be close?","Using the 1-month price history, describe how the fund moved, and whether today''s gap is unusual given that history. What can you not tell?","Pick one tokenised ETF on the board. Why might its quoted price differ from the value of the shares it tracks right now?"],"expectedReasoning":["Reads NAV, price and timestamps correctly","Computes the premium or discount with the right formula","Separates timing gaps from real premiums","Links market hours to stale or estimated prices for tokenised ETFs"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Quotes NAV and price correctly from the pack","Computes premium or discount as price minus NAV divided by NAV","Identifies the fund as an index fund holding crypto assets","Explains that NAV is the value of holdings per share and price is the last agreed trade","Notes that a tokenised ETF''s underlying shares do not trade outside market hours"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the NAV and price timestamps","Cites the 1-month price history with dates","Cites a tokenised ETF row and its market status","Notes the provenance of each figure"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains how a time gap between NAV and price can create an apparent premium","Uses the price history to judge whether today is unusual","Keeps the gap as an observation rather than a forecast","Explains why a closed underlying market makes a token price an estimate"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says the history does not include past NAV gaps, if that is so","Avoids reading a premium as confidence or a discount as a bargain","Names missing information such as redemption rules","Qualifies conclusions drawn from one month"]}],"keyPoints":["nav is the value of the holdings per share at a stated time","the traded price is the last agreed trade","premium or discount equals price minus nav divided by nav","different timestamps can explain a gap","a closed stock market means the token price is an estimate","one month of history is a short sample"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Expressing the gap in price units as well as percent","Concluding the gap is too small to interpret, with the reason given","Choosing a different tokenised ETF for the last question"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["nav-equals-price","stale-price-is-current","observation-is-interpretation"]},"hints":["Write down NAV, price and the time of each before computing anything.","If the two times differ, could the members have moved in between? For the ETF, check whether its home market is open.","A full answer gives the premium or discount with the formula, says whether timing could explain it, places it against the 1-month history, and explains that a closed market makes a tokenised ETF price an estimate."]},{"id":"ex.wealth.yield-apy","skillIds":["wealth.yield-apy"],"kind":"scenario","title":"What does this APY mean?","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Read APY and ROI figures for a yield vault and for tokenised ETFs, explain why a short-window APY can mislead, and say where vault yield comes from and what can go wrong.","evidenceRequirements":["wealth:board","wealth:fund:Morpho-Earn"],"questions":["State the USDC vault''s 7-day and 1-month APY and its yield basis. What does ''net excluding rewards'' mean?","Find a tokenised ETF row with a 7-day APY. Compare it with that ETF''s 1-year ROI. Why are they so different?","Where does the vault''s yield come from, and what are three ways it could fall or be lost?","Write one sentence describing the vault''s yield that the data supports and one it does not."],"expectedReasoning":["Separates APY (scaled-up) from ROI (actual past period)","Explains annualising a single week","Names the source of vault yield and its risks","Avoids describing any APY as guaranteed"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Quotes the vault APY figures correctly","Explains that net excluding rewards means after fees and without token rewards","Explains that a 7-day APY scales one week up to a year","Identifies lending interest as the source of the vault yield","Names contract, borrower or liquidity, and rate-change risks"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the vault row and its timestamps","Cites an ETF''s 7-day APY and 1-year ROI from the board","Notes the yield basis field","Notes the provenance of the board"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why a short window is fair for steady interest but not for price moves","Uses the ETF comparison to show the scaling problem","Links each risk to how the yield could change","Keeps the supported sentence to what was observed"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says an APY is a description of the past, not a promise","Notes that rates can change week to week","Names information not in the pack, such as the vault''s lending exposure","Avoids certainty about next year''s yield"]}],"keyPoints":["apy scales a short window up to a year","roi is the actual return over a past period","a 7-day apy for a price-moving fund is not a yield","vault yield comes from interest paid by borrowers","net excluding rewards means after fees and without token rewards","an apy is not a promise"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Using the 24-hour APY alongside the 7-day figure","Naming a different trio of risks, if each is a real risk of a lending vault","Choosing any ETF row for the comparison"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["apy-is-guaranteed","short-window-apy-is-annual","past-return-predicts"]},"hints":["Look at the vault''s APY columns and its yield basis label first.","An APY takes a short window and repeats it for a year. What happens if that window was a week when a share price jumped?","A full answer quotes the vault APYs and basis, contrasts an ETF''s huge 7-day APY with its 1-year ROI, names borrower interest as the source of vault yield with contract, liquidity and rate risks, and calls every APY a description of the past."]},{"id":"ex.wealth.dca","skillIds":["wealth.dca"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Read a DCA simulation","difficulty":3,"learningObjective":"Read two weekly DCA simulations, check the window they really cover, compare them with the price path, and explain why they do not predict anything.","evidenceRequirements":["wealth:dca:vVOO:1Y","wealth:dca:vQQQ:1Y","wealth:price:vQQQ:3M"],"questions":["State the total simulated return for each fund and the first and last dates in each series. Does the window match the ''1Y'' label and today''s date?","Which simulation ended higher, and where along the way did each one dip or peak?","Using the 3-month price history, would a lump sum at the start of that period have done better or worse than weekly buying? What does that depend on?","Why do these results not tell you what a weekly plan started today would return?"],"expectedReasoning":["Reads the simulation totals and dates correctly","Notices any gap between the last point and today","Explains that DCA versus lump sum depends on the path","Refuses to use past simulated returns as a forecast"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Quotes each simulation''s total return correctly as a percentage","Reads the first and last dates of each series correctly","Explains that DCA buys more units at lower prices","States that neither DCA nor lump sum always wins","Does not present the simulation as a plan''s expected return"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites both simulations by fund id","Cites specific points such as the lowest return and its date","Uses the 3-month price history for the lump-sum comparison","Notes provenance and the date the data was fetched"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Compares the two paths, not just the end points","Links the lump-sum comparison to rising or falling prices in the period","Explains how a different start date would change the result","Keeps observation and interpretation separate"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["States past simulated returns do not predict future returns","Flags any mismatch between the label and the dates covered","Notes that one path is a sample of one","Avoids saying which approach will do better next"]}],"keyPoints":["the simulation is one weekly equal-contribution path","check the dates the simulation actually covers","dca buys more units when the price is lower","whether dca beats a lump sum depends on the price path","a different start date gives a different result","past simulated returns do not predict future returns"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Comparing peak-to-end rather than start-to-end","Concluding the lump-sum comparison cannot be done exactly from the data given","Expressing returns as fractions if converted correctly"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["past-return-predicts","dca-always-wins","missing-data-is-neutral"]},"hints":["Start with the dates: when does each series begin and end, and how far is the last point from today?","Did the price mostly rise, mostly fall, or dip and recover in the window? That decides whether spreading purchases helped.","A full answer gives both totals and their dates, flags any window gap, ties DCA versus lump sum to the path, and says the simulation is one past path that does not predict a plan started now."]},{"id":"ex.wealth.incentives","skillIds":["wealth.incentives"],"kind":"scenario","title":"Paid-for volume","difficulty":4,"learningObjective":"Read a venue''s public incentive rules and reward feed, explain how they inflate volume, and say what is missing to judge a reward''s value.","evidenceRequirements":["rewards:incentives","rewards:feed","orderbook:perps:ANT-USD"],"questions":["Which markets carry a points multiplier, and how much volume earns one reward box? How are taker and maker volume weighted?","Is ANT-USD perpetual boosted? Why does that make its volume weak evidence of demand for ANT?","What does the public reward feed show, what filter applies to it, and why is it a biased sample?","What would you need to know to work out the expected value of one box? Is it in the data?"],"expectedReasoning":["Reads multipliers, volume per box and weights correctly","Links incentives to volume that is not organic demand","Identifies the feed filter as survivorship bias","Names unpublished probabilities as the missing piece"],"rubric":{"dimensions":[{"key":"factual","label":"Factual accuracy","maxPoints":35,"criteria":["Lists the boosted markets and multiplier correctly","States the volume per box and the taker and maker weights correctly","Works out how much maker volume one box needs at the stated weight","Explains that rewards pay people to trade, raising volume","States that drop probabilities are not published"]},{"key":"evidence","label":"Use of evidence","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Cites the incentive figures and the season or week dates","Cites feed items with levels and reward amounts","Cites the feed''s filter note","Refers to the ANT-USD perpetual book as the boosted market"]},{"key":"reasoning","label":"Quality of reasoning","maxPoints":25,"criteria":["Explains why paid-for volume can vanish when a campaign ends","Explains why a feed of large wins hides the typical result","Connects the missing probabilities to an unknown expected value","Keeps observation and interpretation separate"]},{"key":"uncertainty","label":"Handling uncertainty","maxPoints":15,"criteria":["Says how much of the volume is reward-driven is unknown","Refuses to estimate a typical reward from the feed","Notes the rules can change between seasons","Avoids any suggestion to trade for rewards"]}],"keyPoints":["points multipliers reward volume on chosen markets","one box per fixed amount of counted volume","maker volume counts at a lower weight than taker volume","incentive volume is not the same as demand","the feed only lists large wins so it is survivorship biased","drop probabilities are not published","the expected value of a box cannot be computed"],"acceptableAlternatives":["Expressing the maker requirement as a multiple of the taker requirement","Using reward bands to give a range rather than an expected value, if the range is labelled as such","Discussing week pool points as a separate incentive"],"commonMisconceptionIds":["incentive-volume-is-demand","feed-shows-typical-reward","volume-implies-direction","missing-data-is-neutral"]},"hints":["Start with the rules: list the multipliers, the volume per box, and the taker and maker weights.","If traders are paid per unit of volume, what happens to volume on that market, and what happens when the payment stops? Then ask which wins the feed leaves out.","A full answer lists the boosted markets and box rules, works out the maker volume per box, says ANT-USD volume is partly paid for, calls the feed survivorship-biased because of its filter, and says the expected value of a box is unknown because probabilities are not published."]}],"rubricNotes":"Reward answers that name what kind of fund each number comes from before interpreting it, and that quote the board, simulation and incentive figures with their times. Penalise treating an APY, ROI or simulated DCA return as a forecast, treating fund count as diversification, treating a blank fee as zero, and reading incentive-driven volume or a filtered win feed as typical. Arithmetic (premium, fee drag, HHI, maker volume per box) should be right to rounding.","recommendedNext":"This is the last module on the path. Build or revise a practice portfolio and have its rationale graded, then revisit ''Indexes'' for weighting and concentration, and ''Reasoning'' to sharpen how you state uncertainty and invalidation."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set learning_path_id = excluded.learning_path_id, sequence = excluded.sequence, content = excluded.content;

commit;
